"We are given the following information:\n\n- Krystian works in the library from **Monday to Friday** (5 days).\n- He borrows an **average of 40 books per day**.\n- **Every Friday**, he borrows **40% more** than the daily average.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of books borrowed on a regular day (Monday to Thursday)\n\nThe average is 40 books per day, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Books per regular day} = 40\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of books borrowed on Friday\n\nOn Friday, he borrows 40% more than the average:\n\n$$\n\\text{Books on Friday} = 40 + (40\\% \\times 40) = 40 + 16 = 56\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate total books borrowed in a week\n\n- Monday to Thursday: 4 days \u00d7 40 books/day = 160 books\n- Friday: 56 books\n\n$$\n\\text{Total books in a week} = 160 + 56 = 216\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 216**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many days Herman feeds the birds\nHerman feeds the birds in **December, January, and February**.  \n- December has **31 days**  \n- January has **31 days**  \n- February has **28 days** (assuming it's not a leap year)  \n\nTotal days = 31 + 31 + 28 = **90 days**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: How much food does Herman use per day?\nHe feeds the birds **1/2 cup in the morning** and **1/2 cup in the afternoon**.  \nSo, per day:  \n$$\n\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 1 \\text{ cup per day}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total food needed for 90 days\n$$\n90 \\text{ days} \\times 1 \\text{ cup/day} = 90 \\text{ cups}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 90**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Base pay**: $80 per day  \n- **Work hours per day**: 8 hours  \n- **Bonus**: $20 per day (if John works hard)  \n- **Extra work time**: 2 hours longer per day (so total work time becomes 10 hours per day)\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total earnings per day if John earns the bonus\n\n$$\n\\text{Total earnings} = \\text{Base pay} + \\text{Bonus} = 80 + 20 = 100 \\text{ dollars per day}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total work time per day\n\n$$\n\\text{Total work time} = 8 + 2 = 10 \\text{ hours per day}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate hourly wage\n\n$$\n\\text{Hourly wage} = \\frac{\\text{Total earnings}}{\\text{Total work time}} = \\frac{100}{10} = 10 \\text{ dollars per hour}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 10**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Sally earns **$6 per day**.\n- Bob earns **$4 per day**.\n- Both save **half** of what they earn for their trip.\n- We are to find how much **both** will have saved **after a year** (assuming 365 days in a year).\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate Sally's daily savings\nSally saves half of her daily earnings:\n$$\n\\text{Sally's daily savings} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 = 3 \\text{ dollars per day}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Bob's daily savings\nBob saves half of his daily earnings:\n$$\n\\text{Bob's daily savings} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 = 2 \\text{ dollars per day}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate total savings per year\n\n- **Sally's annual savings**:\n$$\n3 \\text{ dollars/day} \\times 365 \\text{ days} = 1095 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n- **Bob's annual savings**:\n$$\n2 \\text{ dollars/day} \\times 365 \\text{ days} = 730 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total savings for both\n\n$$\n\\text{Total savings} = 1095 + 730 = 1825 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 1825**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the cost of the beef.**\n\n- John orders **1000 pounds** of beef.\n- The price per pound of beef is **$8**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost of beef} = 1000 \\times 8 = 8000 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the amount of chicken ordered.**\n\n- The problem says John orders **twice that much chicken** as beef.\n- Beef = 1000 pounds, so chicken = $ 2 \\times 1000 = 2000 $ pounds.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the cost of the chicken.**\n\n- Chicken = 2000 pounds.\n- Price per pound of chicken = **$3**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost of chicken} = 2000 \\times 3 = 6000 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Add the costs of beef and chicken to get the total cost.**\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 8000 + 6000 = 14000 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 14000**"
