"Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Total number of slices**\n\nAlex has 2 cakes, each cut into 8 slices:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 8 = 16 \\text{ slices}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Slices given to friends**\n\nA fourth of the slices are given away to friends:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4} \\times 16 = 4 \\text{ slices}\n$$\n\nRemaining slices after giving to friends:\n\n$$\n16 - 4 = 12 \\text{ slices}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Slices given to family**\n\nA third of the remaining slices are given to family:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} \\times 12 = 4 \\text{ slices}\n$$\n\nRemaining slices after giving to family:\n\n$$\n12 - 4 = 8 \\text{ slices}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Slices eaten by Alex**\n\nAlex eats 3 slices:\n\n$$\n8 - 3 = 5 \\text{ slices left}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{5}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 5**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- 26 people took **wine**.\n- 22 people took **soda**.\n- 17 people took **both** drinks (wine and soda).\n\nWe are to find the **total number of people** at the gathering, assuming that **each person could have taken one or more drinks**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the principle of inclusion and exclusion\n\nLet:\n- $ W $ = set of people who took wine\n- $ S $ = set of people who took soda\n\nWe are given:\n- $ |W| = 26 $\n- $ |S| = 22 $\n- $ |W \\cap S| = 17 $\n\nThe total number of people who took either wine or soda (or both) is:\n\n$$\n|W \\cup S| = |W| + |S| - |W \\cap S|\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\n$$\n|W \\cup S| = 26 + 22 - 17 = 31\n$$\n\nSo, **31 people** took either wine, soda, or both.\n\nSince the problem says **each person could have taken one or more drinks**, and we are told that **all** people at the gathering took **at least one** of the drinks, the total number of people at the gathering is the same as the number of people who took either drink.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{31}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 31**"
