"We are given the following:\n\n- Tom needs to lower a **rope down 6 stories**.\n- Each **story is 10 feet**, so the total height is:\n  $$\n  6 \\times 10 = 60 \\text{ feet}\n  $$\n- The only rope available is **20 feet long**.\n- When **lapping** or **lapping together**, you **lose 25%** of the rope length.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how much rope is needed\n\nTom needs **60 feet** of rope.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how much rope is lost when lashing\n\nEach 20-foot rope is **25% shorter** when lashed together. That means:\n\n- **25% of 20 feet** = $ 0.25 \\times 20 = 5 $ feet lost.\n- So, **each 20-foot rope is only 15 feet usable** when lashed.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: How many 15-foot ropes are needed to get 60 feet?\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{15} = 4\n$$\n\nSo, **4 ropes** are needed.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{4}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 4**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Samuel **swept the porch** and **cleared nine-tenths of the dust particles** from it.\n- After sweeping, **his shoes left 223 dust particles** behind.\n- After he **walked across the porch**, there were **331 dust particles** on the porch.\n\nWe are to find: **How many dust particles were on the porch before Samuel swept?**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let the original number of dust particles on the porch be $ x $.\n\nSamuel swept and **cleared nine-tenths** of the dust, so he **left one-tenth** of the dust on the porch.\n\nSo, after sweeping, the number of dust particles left on the porch is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}x\n$$\n\nThen, **his shoes left 223 more dust particles** behind, so the total number of dust particles on the porch after he walked is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}x + 223\n$$\n\nWe are told that this total is **331**:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}x + 223 = 331\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}x + 223 = 331\n$$\n\nSubtract 223 from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}x = 331 - 223 = 108\n$$\n\nNow multiply both sides by 10:\n\n$$\nx = 108 \\times 10 = 1080\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1080}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 1080**"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Total game time\nThe total duration of the soccer game is **90 minutes**.\n\n### Step 2: Time Mark played\n- First, Mark played **20 minutes**.\n- Then, he rested.\n- After resting, he played **35 minutes**.\n\nSo, the total time Mark **played** is:\n$$\n20 + 35 = 55 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Time on the sideline\nTo find how long Mark was on the sideline, subtract the time he played from the total game time:\n$$\n90 - 55 = 35 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{35}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 35**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Power of TV**: 125 watts = 0.125 kilowatts (kW)\n- **Daily usage**: 4 hours\n- **Cost of electricity**: 14 cents per kilowatt-hour (kWh)\n- **Time period**: 1 week = 7 days\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate daily energy consumption\n\n$$\n\\text{Daily energy} = \\text{Power} \\times \\text{Time} = 0.125 \\, \\text{kW} \\times 4 \\, \\text{hours} = 0.5 \\, \\text{kWh}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate weekly energy consumption\n\n$$\n\\text{Weekly energy} = 0.5 \\, \\text{kWh/day} \\times 7 \\, \\text{days} = 3.5 \\, \\text{kWh}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the cost in cents\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost} = 3.5 \\, \\text{kWh} \\times 14 \\, \\text{cents/kWh} = 49 \\, \\text{cents}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 49**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Archie loses **60%** of his marbles into the street.\n- Of the **remaining** marbles, he loses **half** down a sewer.\n- After these losses, he has **20 marbles left**.\n\nWe need to find out how many marbles he **started with**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let the original number of marbles be $ x $.\n\n- **First loss**: He loses 60% of the marbles, so he has:\n  $$\n  x - 0.6x = 0.4x\n  $$\n  left after the first loss.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: He loses **half** of the remaining marbles down the sewer.\n\nSo, he loses:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.4x = 0.2x\n$$\nHe keeps:\n$$\n0.4x - 0.2x = 0.2x\n$$\n\nThis is the number of marbles he has left, which is given as **20**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Set up the equation\n\n$$\n0.2x = 20\n$$\n\nSolve for $ x $:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{20}{0.2} = 100\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{100}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 100**"
"Step 1: Calculate Adam's daily earnings before taxes.  \nAdam earns **$40 per day**.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the amount deducted as taxes.  \n10% of $40 is:  \n$$\n0.10 \\times 40 = 4\n$$  \nSo, $4 is deducted as taxes each day.\n\nStep 3: Calculate Adam's net daily earnings after taxes.  \n$$\n40 - 4 = 36\n$$  \nAdam earns **$36 per day** after taxes.\n\nStep 4: Calculate his total earnings after taxes over 30 days.  \n$$\n36 \\times 30 = 1080\n$$\n\n**Answer: 1080**"
"Let's solve this step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Find the area of each wall\n\n**Wall 1 and Wall 2:**\n- Dimensions: 3 meters wide \u00d7 2 meters tall  \n- Area = 3 \u00d7 2 = **6 square meters**  \n- There are 2 such walls, so total area = 2 \u00d7 6 = **12 square meters**\n\n**Wall 3:**\n- Dimensions: 5 meters wide \u00d7 2 meters tall  \n- Area = 5 \u00d7 2 = **10 square meters**\n\n**Wall 4:**\n- Dimensions: 4 meters wide \u00d7 2 meters tall  \n- Area = 4 \u00d7 2 = **8 square meters**\n\n### Step 2: Total area of all walls\n\nTotal area = 12 (from walls 1 and 2) + 10 (wall 3) + 8 (wall 4)  \n= **30 square meters**\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the number of paint cans needed\n\nEach can of paint covers **2 square meters**.\n\nNumber of cans = Total area \u00f7 Area per can  \n= 30 \u00f7 2 = **15 cans**\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 15**"
"We are told:\n\n- Jeremy listened to **five fewer songs** yesterday than today.\n- Yesterday, he listened to **9 songs**.\n- We need to find how many songs he listened to in **two days**.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1:** Let the number of songs Jeremy listened to **today** be $ x $.\n\nThen, the number of songs he listened to **yesterday** is $ x - 5 $.\n\nWe are told that **yesterday**, he listened to **9 songs**:\n\n$$\nx - 5 = 9\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $ x $:\n\n$$\nx = 9 + 5 = 14\n$$\n\nSo, Jeremy listened to **14 songs today** and **9 songs yesterday**.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3:** Add the number of songs from both days:\n\n$$\n14 + 9 = 23\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 23**"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n---\n\n**Initial time:**  \nBrian can hold his breath for **10 seconds**.\n\n**Week 1 (after a week):**  \nHe **doubles** the time:  \n$$ 10 \\times 2 = 20 \\text{ seconds} $$\n\n**Week 2 (after another week):**  \nHe **doubles it again** from the previous week:  \n$$ 20 \\times 2 = 40 \\text{ seconds} $$\n\n**Week 3 (final week):**  \nHe **increases it by 50%** from the previous week:  \n$$ 40 \\times 1.5 = 60 \\text{ seconds} $$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nBrian can now hold his breath for **60 seconds**.\n\n**Answer: 60**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. The farmer has **twice as many pigs as cows**.\n2. The farmer has **4 more cows than goats**.\n3. The total number of animals is **56**.\n\nLet\u2019s define variables:\n\n- Let $ g $ = number of **goats**\n- Then, number of **cows** = $ g + 4 $\n- Number of **pigs** = $ 2 \\times $ number of cows = $ 2(g + 4) $\n\nNow, the total number of animals is:\n\n$$\ng + (g + 4) + 2(g + 4) = 56\n$$\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng + (g + 4) + 2(g + 4) = g + g + 4 + 2g + 8 = 4g + 12\n$$\n\nSet equal to 56:\n\n$$\n4g + 12 = 56\n$$\n\nSubtract 12 from both sides:\n\n$$\n4g = 44\n$$\n\nDivide by 4:\n\n$$\ng = 11\n$$\n\n**Answer: 11**"
"Let's solve this step by step:\n\n1. **Total rolls Chandler needs to sell:** 12  \n2. **Rolls sold to grandmother:** 3  \n3. **Rolls sold to uncle:** 4  \n4. **Rolls sold to neighbor:** 3  \n\nNow, add the rolls already sold:  \n$$\n3 + 4 + 3 = 10\n$$\n\nSubtract the number of rolls already sold from the total needed:  \n$$\n12 - 10 = 2\n$$\n\n**Answer: 2**"
"We are told that Jackson\u2019s mom packs him a **peanut butter and jelly sandwich** on **Wednesdays and Fridays** for his school lunch.\n\n### Step 1: Total number of weeks in the school year  \nThere are **36 weeks** of school.\n\n### Step 2: Number of days per week he gets a sandwich  \nHe gets a sandwich on **Wednesday** and **Friday** \u2192 that's **2 days per week**.\n\nSo, the total number of sandwiches he would get in a full school year is:  \n$$\n36 \\text{ weeks} \\times 2 \\text{ sandwiches/week} = 72 \\text{ sandwiches}\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Subtract the days he missed  \nHe missed **1 Wednesday** and **2 Fridays** \u2192 that's a total of **3 days**.\n\nSo, the number of sandwiches he **did not eat** is **3**.\n\n### Step 4: Subtract the missed days from the total  \n$$\n72 - 3 = 69\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:  \n**69** peanut butter and jelly sandwiches.\n\n**Answer: 69**"
"Step 1: Calculate the total number of cards Punger has.  \nHe buys 60 packs, and each pack has 7 cards:  \n$$\n60 \\times 7 = 420 \\text{ cards}\n$$\n\nStep 2: Determine how many pages he needs.  \nEach page can hold 10 cards:  \n$$\n420 \\div 10 = 42 \\text{ pages}\n$$\n\nAnswer: 42"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Remy paid **$20,700** for **150 pieces** of furniture.\n- The **cost of a piece of furniture is $134**.\n- The company overcharged Remy, so they will reimburse him the difference.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total cost if the price per piece was correct.\n\n$$\n\\text{Correct total cost} = 150 \\times 134 = 20,100\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the overcharge amount.\n\n$$\n\\text{Overcharge} = \\text{Amount paid} - \\text{Correct total cost} = 20,700 - 20,100 = 600\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{600}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 600**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. Seth bought some boxes of oranges.\n2. He gave **1 box** to his mother.\n3. Then he gave away **half of the remaining boxes**.\n4. After that, he had **4 boxes left**.\n\nWe need to find out how many boxes he originally bought.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let the total number of boxes Seth originally bought be **x**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: He gave 1 box to his mother.\n\nSo, the number of boxes left after that is:  \n**x - 1**\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: He gave away half of the remaining boxes.\n\nSo, he gave away:  \n**(x - 1) / 2**\n\nThis means the number of boxes he **kept** is:  \n**(x - 1) / 2**\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: After giving away half, he had 4 boxes left.\n\nSo, we set up the equation:  \n**(x - 1) / 2 = 4**\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Solve the equation.\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:  \n**x - 1 = 8**\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:  \n**x = 9**\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:  \n**Answer: 9**"
"We are given a grid with:\n\n- **10 rows**\n- **15 squares per row**\n\nSo, the total number of squares in the grid is:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 15 = 150 \\text{ squares}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Color 4 rows of 6 squares in the middle with red\n\nThis is a bit ambiguous, but we interpret it as:\n\n- Marla colors **4 rows**, each with **6 squares** in the **middle** of the grid.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the grid has **15 squares per row**, so coloring **6 squares in the middle** of a row would mean coloring **6 squares** in the middle of each of the **4 rows**.\n\nSo, the number of red squares is:\n\n$$\n4 \\text{ rows} \\times 6 \\text{ squares} = 24 \\text{ red squares}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Color all squares on the first 2 and last 2 rows with blue\n\n- First 2 rows: 2 rows \u00d7 15 squares = 30 squares\n- Last 2 rows: 2 rows \u00d7 15 squares = 30 squares\n- Total blue squares: 30 + 30 = **60 squares**\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Color the rest with green\n\nTotal squares = 150  \nRed squares = 24  \nBlue squares = 60\n\nSo, green squares = total - red - blue\n\n$$\n150 - 24 - 60 = 66\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{66}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 66**"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Given:**\n\n- **Quiz structure:**\n  - 5 rounds.\n  - Each round has **5 questions**.\n  - **Total questions**: $ 5 \\text{ rounds} \\times 5 \\text{ questions} = 25 $ questions.\n- **Scoring:**\n  - **2 points per correct answer**.\n  - **4 bonus points** if a student **correctly answers all questions in a round**.\n- **James's performance:**\n  - Missed **1 question**.\n  - So, he got **24 questions correct**.\n  - He **missed 1 question**, so he **did not get the bonus** for that round.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Points from correct answers**\n\n- James got 24 questions correct.\n- Each correct answer = 2 points.\n- Total points from correct answers:  \n  $ 24 \\times 2 = 48 $ points.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Bonus points**\n\n- There are 5 rounds.\n- James **missed 1 question**, so he **missed 1 round**.\n- That means he **did not get the bonus** for that round.\n- So, he **got the bonus** for **4 rounds**.\n- Bonus per round = 4 points.\n- Total bonus points:  \n  $ 4 \\text{ rounds} \\times 4 = 16 $ points.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Total points**\n\n- Points from correct answers: 48\n- Bonus points: 16  \n  $ 48 + 16 = 64 $\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{64}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 64**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. Catriona has **8 goldfish**.\n2. She has **4 more angelfish than goldfish**.\n3. She has **twice as many guppies as angelfish**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of angelfish\n\nShe has 4 more angelfish than goldfish, and she has 8 goldfish.\n\n$$\n\\text{Angelfish} = 8 + 4 = 12\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of guppies\n\nShe has twice as many guppies as angelfish.\n\n$$\n\\text{Guppies} = 2 \\times 12 = 24\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add all the fish together\n\n$$\n\\text{Total fish} = \\text{Goldfish} + \\text{Angelfish} + \\text{Guppies} = 8 + 12 + 24 = 44\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 44**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Carl takes out 12 marbles.\n\n- He starts with an unknown number of marbles, but we don't need to know the total initially. We just track what happens to the marbles he takes out.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: He drops 12 marbles, and **1/2** get lost.\n\n- So, **6 marbles** are lost (because 12 \u00d7 1/2 = 6).\n- That means **6 marbles remain** with him.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Carl takes out **10 more marbles**.\n\n- Now he has **6 (from first 12)** + **10 (new ones)** = **16 marbles** in total that he has in his hands.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: His mother comes home with a **bag of 25 marbles**.\n\n- These 25 marbles are **added to the jar** (since he can't find the lost ones, he puts the new ones in the jar).\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: How many marbles is Carl going to put in the jar?\n\n- The **lost marbles** (6) are **not** put back in the jar.\n- The **10 marbles** he took out after the first 12 are **still with him** (he didn't lose them).\n- The **25 marbles** from his mother are **added to the jar**.\n\nSo, the marbles he puts in the jar are:\n\n- The **10 marbles** he took out after the first 12\n- The **25 marbles** from his mother\n\n**Total = 10 + 25 = 35**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 35**"
"We are given the reading rates of three people: **Rene**, **Lulu**, and **Cherry**. We are to find how many pages they have read in **240 minutes**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the reading rate per minute for each person.\n\n- **Rene** reads 30 pages in 60 minutes \u2192  \n  $ \\frac{30}{60} = 0.5 $ pages per minute\n\n- **Lulu** reads 27 pages in 60 minutes \u2192  \n  $ \\frac{27}{60} = 0.45 $ pages per minute\n\n- **Cherry** reads 25 pages in 60 minutes \u2192  \n  $ \\frac{25}{60} \\approx 0.4167 $ pages per minute\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Multiply each person's rate by the total time (240 minutes).\n\n- **Rene**: $ 0.5 \\times 240 = 120 $ pages  \n- **Lulu**: $ 0.45 \\times 240 = 108 $ pages  \n- **Cherry**: $ 0.4167 \\times 240 \\approx 100 $ pages\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the pages read by all three.\n\n$$\n120 + 108 + 100 = 328\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n**Answer: 328**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Sonja uses **2 ounces of oil** for every **8 ounces of peanuts**.\n- The total weight of her last batch of peanut butter is **20 ounces**.\n- We are to find how many **ounces of oil** she used.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the ratio\n\nThe ratio of **oil to peanuts** is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Oil} : \\text{Peanuts} = 2 : 8 = 1 : 4\n$$\n\nThis means for every **1 ounce of oil**, there is **4 ounces of peanuts**.\n\nSo, the total amount of peanut butter is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Oil} + \\text{Peanuts} = 1 + 4 = 5 \\text{ parts}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the value of each part\n\nThe total weight of the batch is 20 ounces, and this is divided into 5 parts:\n\n$$\n\\text{Each part} = \\frac{20}{5} = 4 \\text{ ounces}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the amount of oil\n\nSince oil is 1 part:\n\n$$\n\\text{Oil} = 1 \\times 4 = 4 \\text{ ounces}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 4}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The Skipper sailed **due east** for **20 hours** at a speed of **30 km/h**.\n- After that, the **wind storm** blew the ship **westward**.\n- After the storm, the ship was **only one-third of the way** to the destination.\n- We are to find how many kilometers the ship was **blown westward** by the storm.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the distance sailed east\n\nThe Skipper sailed east for 20 hours at 30 km/h:\n\n$$\n\\text{Distance sailed east} = 20 \\times 30 = 600 \\text{ km}\n$$\n\nThis is the **first part** of the journey.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Let the total distance to the destination be $ D $\n\nAfter the storm, the ship was **one-third of the way** to the destination. That means:\n\n$$\n\\text{Distance from start to current position} = \\frac{1}{3}D\n$$\n\nBut the ship had already sailed **600 km east**, and then was blown **westward** by the storm. So the **net distance** from the start is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Net distance} = 600 - \\text{distance blown west}\n$$\n\nWe are told this net distance is **one-third of the total distance**:\n\n$$\n600 - x = \\frac{1}{3}D\n$$\n\nWhere $ x $ is the distance the ship was blown **westward** by the storm.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Express total distance in terms of the net distance\n\nFrom the equation above:\n\n$$\n600 - x = \\frac{1}{3}D \\Rightarrow D = 3(600 - x)\n$$\n\nSo the total distance is:\n\n$$\nD = 1"
"We are given a series of relationships between the weights of Karen's tote bag and Kevin's briefcase. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Define variables**\n\nLet:\n- $ K $ = weight of Karen\u2019s tote bag\n- $ B $ = weight of Kevin\u2019s empty briefcase\n- $ L $ = weight of Kevin\u2019s laptop\n- $ P $ = weight of Kevin\u2019s work papers\n- $ T $ = total weight of Kevin\u2019s full briefcase (i.e., $ B + L + P $)\n\nWe are told:\n- Karen\u2019s tote bag weighs **8 pounds** \u2192 $ K = 8 $\n- **When the briefcase is empty**, it is **twice the weight of Karen\u2019s tote**:\n  $$\n  B = 2K = 2 \\times 8 = 16 \\text{ pounds}\n  $$\n- When Kevin puts his **laptop and work papers** in the briefcase, the **total weight** of the briefcase is **twice the weight of Karen\u2019s tote**:\n  $$\n  T = 2K = 2 \\times 8 = 16 \\text{ pounds}\n  $$\n  So:\n  $$\n  B + L + P = 16\n  $$\n  But we already know $ B = 16 $, so:\n  $$\n  16 + L + P = 16 \\Rightarrow L + P = 0\n  $$\n  Wait, that can't be right. Let's re-examine the problem.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Re-express the key relationships**\n\nWe are told:\n- **When the briefcase is empty**, it is **twice the weight of Karen\u2019s tote**:\n  $$\n  B = 2K = 2 \\times 8 = 16 \\text{ pounds}\n  $$\n-"
"Let's define the variables:\n\n- Let $ a $ be the cost of **1 dozen apples**.\n- Let $ b $ be the cost of **1 bunch of bananas**.\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following equations:\n\n1. Tony paid $7 for 2 dozen apples and 1 bunch of bananas:\n   $$\n   2a + b = 7 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n   $$\n\n2. Arnold paid $5 for 1 dozen apples and 1 bunch of bananas:\n   $$\n   a + b = 5 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n   $$\n\nNow, solve the system of equations.\n\n### Step 1: Subtract Equation 2 from Equation 1\n$$\n(2a + b) - (a + b) = 7 - 5\n$$\n$$\n2a + b - a - b = 2\n$$\n$$\na = 2\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Substitute $ a = 2 $ into Equation 2\n$$\n2 + b = 5\n$$\n$$\nb = 3\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{3}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 3**"
"Step 1: Stu has 9 books.  \nStep 2: Albert has 4 times as many books as Stu.  \nSo, Albert has:  \n$$ 4 \\times 9 = 36 \\text{ books} $$  \nStep 3: Total number of books is:  \n$$ 9 + 36 = 45 $$\n\nAnswer: 45"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- There are **8 runners** in the race.\n- The **first 5 runners** finish the race in **8 hours**.\n- The **remaining 3 runners** finish the race **2 hours later**, i.e., in **8 + 2 = 10 hours**.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total time taken by all runners.\n\n- The first 5 runners each take **8 hours**.\n- The remaining 3 runners each take **10 hours**.\n\nSo, the total time is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total time} = (5 \\times 8) + (3 \\times 10)\n$$\n\n$$\n= 40 + 30 = 70 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{70}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 70**"
"Let's break the problem into steps and solve it carefully.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Find the length of Keaton's ladder**\n\n- Keaton's ladder is **30 feet** long.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Find the length of Reece's ladder**\n\n- Reece's ladder is **4 feet shorter** than Keaton's.\n- So, Reece's ladder length = 30 feet - 4 feet = **26 feet**.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Total number of times both workers climbed the ladders**\n\n- Keaton climbed the ladder **20 times**.\n- Reece climbed the ladder **15 times**.\n- Total climbs = 20 + 15 = **35 climbs**.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Convert ladder lengths to inches**\n\n- 1 foot = 12 inches\n- Keaton's ladder: 30 feet \u00d7 12 = **360 inches**\n- Reece's ladder: 26 feet \u00d7 12 = **312 inches**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Total length of ladders climbed**\n\n- Total length = (360 inches \u00d7 20) + (312 inches \u00d7 15)\n- = 7200 inches + 4680 inches\n- = **11,880 inches**\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n**Answer: 11880**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Emily's salary: $1,000,000 per year  \n- She has 10 employees, each making $20,000 per year  \n- She wants to raise each employee's salary to $35,000 per year  \n- We are to find **how much of her salary she would need to give to her employees** to ensure they all make $35,000 per year.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total salary increase needed for the employees\n\nEach employee currently makes $20,000, and she wants them to make $35,000.\n\n**Increase per employee** = $35,000 - $20,000 = **$15,000**\n\nThere are 10 employees, so:\n\n**Total increase needed** = 10 \u00d7 $15,000 = **$150,000**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how much of Emily's salary is needed to cover this increase\n\nEmily's total salary is $1,000,000. She will need to **give $150,000** from her salary to raise the employees' salaries.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n**Answer: 150000**"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total cost of the coloring books\n\nLinda bought **2 coloring books** at **$4 each**:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 4 = 8\n$$\n\nSo, the coloring books cost **$8**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the total cost of the peanuts\n\nLinda bought **4 packs of peanuts** at **$1.50 each**:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 1.50 = 6\n$$\n\nSo, the peanuts cost **$6**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Let the cost of the stuffed animal be **x**\n\nWe don't know the price of the stuffed animal, so we'll call it **x**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Add up all the costs\n\nTotal cost = cost of coloring books + cost of peanuts + cost of stuffed animal\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 8 + 6 + x = 14 + x\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Use the information that Linda gave $25 and got no change\n\nThis means the total cost must be exactly **$25** (since she gave $25 and got no change).\n\n$$\n14 + x = 25\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Solve for x\n\n$$\nx = 25 - 14 = 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 11}}\n$$"
"Let's break down Monika's expenses step by step:\n\n1. **Mall:**  \n   She spent **$250**.\n\n2. **Movies:**  \n   She watched **3 movies**, each costing **$24**.  \n   Total cost for movies = $ 3 \\times 24 = 72 $ dollars.\n\n3. **Farmer's market:**  \n   She bought **20 bags of beans**, each costing **$1.25**.  \n   Total cost for beans = $ 20 \\times 1.25 = 25 $ dollars.\n\nNow, add up all the expenses:\n\n- Mall: $250  \n- Movies: $72  \n- Farmer's market: $25  \n\nTotal spent = $ 250 + 72 + 25 = 347 $\n\n**Answer: 347**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Missy has **12 patients**.\n- **One-third** of the patients have **special dietary requirements**.\n- **Standard care patients** take **5 minutes** each.\n- **Special dietary patients** take **20% longer** than standard care.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of patients with special dietary requirements.\n\nOne-third of 12 is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} \\times 12 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, **4 patients** have special dietary requirements.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of standard care patients.\n\n$$\n12 - 4 = 8\n$$\n\nSo, **8 patients** receive standard care.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Time for standard care patients.\n\n$$\n8 \\text{ patients} \\times 5 \\text{ minutes} = 40 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Time for special dietary patients.\n\nSpecial dietary patients take **20% longer** than standard care.\n\n$$\n20\\% \\text{ of } 5 = 0.2 \\times 5 = 1 \\text{ minute}\n$$\n\nSo, each special dietary patient takes:\n\n$$\n5 + 1 = 6 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\nNow, time for 4 special dietary patients:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 6 = 24 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Total time to serve all patients.\n\n$$\n40 \\text{ minutes} + 24 \\text{ minutes} = 64 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 64**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Ronald can grill **15 hamburgers per session**.\n- He needs to cook a total of **115 hamburgers**.\n- He has already cooked **40 hamburgers**.\n\n### Step 1: Find how many hamburgers are left to cook.\n\n$$\n\\text{Hamburgers left} = 115 - 40 = 75\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Find how many sessions are needed to cook the remaining 75 hamburgers.\n\n$$\n\\text{Sessions needed} = \\frac{75}{15} = 5\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 5}}\n$$"
"We are given that TJ ran a 10K race, which is 10 kilometers.\n\nHe ran the **first half** (5 km) in **20 minutes**, and the **second half** (5 km) in **30 minutes**.\n\n### Step 1: Total time\n- Time for first half: 20 minutes\n- Time for second half: 30 minutes  \n- **Total time** = 20 + 30 = **50 minutes**\n\n### Step 2: Total distance\n- Total distance = 10 km\n\n### Step 3: Average time per kilometer\n$$\n\\text{Average time per kilometer} = \\frac{\\text{Total time}}{\\text{Total distance}} = \\frac{50}{10} = 5 \\text{ minutes per kilometer}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 5**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Alison bought **3 large storage tubs** and **6 small storage tubs**.\n- The **total cost** is **$48**.\n- Each **large tub** costs **$6**.\n- We need to find the cost of each **small tub**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let the cost of one small tub be **x**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Write the total cost equation.\n\nThe total cost is the sum of the cost of the large tubs and the small tubs:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 6 + 6 \\times x = 48\n$$\n\n$$\n18 + 6x = 48\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Solve for x.\n\nSubtract 18 from both sides:\n\n$$\n6x = 48 - 18\n$$\n\n$$\n6x = 30\n$$\n\nNow divide both sides by 6:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{30}{6} = 5\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{5}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 5**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. The **day before yesterday** had **50 buyers**.\n2. **Yesterday** had **half the number of buyers** as the day before.\n3. **Today** has **40 more buyers** than **yesterday**.\n4. **No one visited the store on more than one day**.\n\nWe are to find the **total number of buyers** over the **three days**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Day before yesterday\nWe are told:\n> \"The day before had 50 buyers.\"\n\nSo, **Day before yesterday** = **50 buyers**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Yesterday\nWe are told:\n> \"There were half the number of buyers yesterday as there were the day before.\"\n\nSo, **Yesterday** = \u00bd \u00d7 (Day before) = \u00bd \u00d7 50 = **25 buyers**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Today\nWe are told:\n> \"There are 40 more buyers today than yesterday.\"\n\nSo, **Today** = Yesterday + 40 = 25 + 40 = **65 buyers**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total number of buyers over the three days\n\nAdd the number of buyers for each day:\n\n- Day before yesterday: 50\n- Yesterday: 25\n- Today: 65\n\nTotal = 50 + 25 + 65 = **140**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 140**"
"We are given that the store owner adds **80%** to the **wholesale price** to set the **retail price**. The **retail price** of a pair of pants is **$36**.\n\nLet the **wholesale price** be $ x $.\n\nThe **retail price** is the **wholesale price plus 80% of the wholesale price**, which can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Retail Price} = x + 0.8x = 1.8x\n$$\n\nWe are told the retail price is $36, so:\n\n$$\n1.8x = 36\n$$\n\nNow, solve for $ x $:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{36}{1.8} = 20\n$$\n\n**Answer: 20**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Kayla picked 20 apples.**\n- **Caleb picked 5 apples less than Kayla.**\n- **Suraya picked 12 apples more than Caleb.**\n- We are to find: **How many more apples did Suraya pick than Kayla?**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find how many apples Caleb picked.\n\nCaleb picked 5 apples less than Kayla:\n\n$$\n\\text{Caleb's apples} = 20 - 5 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many apples Suraya picked.\n\nSuraya picked 12 apples more than Caleb:\n\n$$\n\\text{Suraya's apples} = 15 + 12 = 27\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find how many more apples Suraya picked than Kayla.\n\n$$\n\\text{Difference} = 27 - 20 = 7\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 7**  \n**Answer: 7**"
