"We are given the following:\n\n- **One barnyard owl makes 5 hoots per minute.**\n- **5 less than 20 hoots per minute are heard coming out of the barn.**\n\nLet\u2019s break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of hoots per minute heard\n\nThe problem says:  \n> \"5 less than 20 hoots per minute are heard coming out of the barn.\"\n\nSo, the number of hoots per minute is:\n\n$$\n20 - 5 = 15 \\text{ hoots per minute}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many owls would make 15 hoots per minute\n\nWe know that **1 owl makes 5 hoots per minute**.\n\nSo, the number of owls is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15 \\text{ hoots per minute}}{5 \\text{ hoots per owl per minute}} = 3 \\text{ owls}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 3}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Hayes does **3 loads of laundry per week**.\n- He uses **1 detergent pod per load**.\n- Detergent pods come in **packs of 39**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of loads per year\n\nThere are 52 weeks in a year.\n\nSo, the number of loads per year is:\n\n$$\n3 \\text{ loads/week} \\times 52 \\text{ weeks} = 156 \\text{ loads}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of packs needed\n\nEach pack contains 39 pods, and he needs 156 pods.\n\n$$\n\\frac{156}{39} = 4\n$$\n\nSo, he needs **4 packs** of detergent pods to do a full year of laundry.\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 4**"
"We start with 336 books in the library.\n\n**On Monday**, 124 books are taken out:  \n$$ 336 - 124 = 212 $$\n\n**On Tuesday**, 22 books are brought back:  \n$$ 212 + 22 = 234 $$\n\n**Final count**: 234 books.\n\nAnswer: 234"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Calculate the total distance driven**\n\nWe are given the following distances:\n\n- **Grocery store:** 8 miles  \n- **Pick up kids from school:** 6 miles  \n- **Drop kids off at soccer practice:** 12 miles  \n- **Get everyone home again:** Twice the distance from the school to soccer practice\n\nFirst, the distance from the school to soccer practice is **12 miles** (from the drop-off point). So, the return trip is **2 \u00d7 12 = 24 miles**.\n\nNow, add all the distances:\n\n$$\n8 + 6 + 12 + 24 = 50 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Calculate the amount of gas needed**\n\nCarla's car gets **25 miles per gallon**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Gallons needed} = \\frac{50 \\text{ miles}}{25 \\text{ miles per gallon}} = 2 \\text{ gallons}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Calculate the cost of gas**\n\nGas costs **$2.50 per gallon**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 2 \\text{ gallons} \\times 2.50 \\text{ dollars per gallon} = 5.00 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{5.00}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 5.00**"
"Let's break this down step by step for each day.\n\n---\n\n### **Monday:**\n- **Time spent writing:** 60 minutes  \n- **Rate:** 1 page every 30 minutes  \n- **Pages written:**  \n  $$\n  \\frac{60 \\text{ minutes}}{30 \\text{ minutes per page}} = 2 \\text{ pages}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### **Tuesday:**\n- **Time spent writing:** 45 minutes  \n- **Rate:** 1 page every 15 minutes  \n- **Pages written:**  \n  $$\n  \\frac{45 \\text{ minutes}}{15 \\text{ minutes per page}} = 3 \\text{ pages}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### **Wednesday:**\n- **Pages written:** 5 pages (given directly)\n\n---\n\n### **Total pages from Monday to Wednesday:**\n$$\n2 \\text{ (Monday)} + 3 \\text{ (Tuesday)} + 5 \\text{ (Wednesday)} = 10 \\text{ pages}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 10**"
