"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Time for the injury to fully heal\n\n- The pain subsided after **3 days**.\n- The injury takes **at least 5 times** that long to fully heal.\n\n$$\n\\text{Time to fully heal} = 5 \\times 3 = 15 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Waiting time before starting to work out again\n\n- After the injury has fully healed, James wants to **wait another 3 days** before starting to work out again.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total time after injury} = 15 + 3 = 18 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Waiting time before lifting heavy again\n\n- After starting to work out again, he wants to **wait 3 weeks** before lifting heavy again.\n\n$$\n3 \\text{ weeks} = 3 \\times 7 = 21 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total time until he can lift heavy again\n\n$$\n\\text{Total time} = 18 + 21 = 39 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 39**"
"We are given that Zion and his two friends each built a **triangular-shaped house of cards**. The triangle has a **base of 40 cm** and a **height of 20 cm**. We are to find the **total area** of all three triangular houses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Area of one triangle\n\nThe formula for the area of a triangle is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 40 \\times 20 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 800 = 400 \\text{ cm}^2\n$$\n\nSo, the area of **one** triangular house is **400 cm\u00b2**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total area for three houses\n\nZion and his two friends built **three** identical triangular houses.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Area} = 3 \\times 400 = 1200 \\text{ cm}^2\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 1200}}\n$$"
"Let's solve the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total number of people on the bus\nThere are **90 people** on the bus.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Number of Dutch people\nWe are told that **3/5** of the people are Dutch.\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of Dutch} = \\frac{3}{5} \\times 90 = 54\n$$\n\nSo, **54 people** are Dutch.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Number of Dutch Americans\nWe are told that **1/2 of the Dutch** are also American (i.e., Dutch Americans).\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of Dutch Americans} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 54 = 27\n$$\n\nSo, **27 people** are Dutch Americans.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Number of Dutch Americans who got window seats\nWe are told that **1/3** of the Dutch Americans got window seats.\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of Dutch Americans with window seats} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 27 = 9\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{9}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 9**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The **Chrysler Building** has **11 more floors** than the **Leeward Center**.\n- Together, they have a **total of 35 floors**.\n\nLet\u2019s define variables:\n\n- Let $ C $ = number of floors in the **Chrysler Building**.\n- Let $ L $ = number of floors in the **Leeward Center**.\n\nFrom the problem:\n\n1. $ C = L + 11 $  (The Chrysler Building has 11 more floors)\n2. $ C + L = 35 $  (Together, they have 35 floors)\n\nNow substitute equation (1) into equation (2):\n\n$$\n(L + 11) + L = 35\n$$\n\nSimplify:\n\n$$\n2L + 11 = 35\n$$\n\nSubtract 11 from both sides:\n\n$$\n2L = 24\n$$\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n$$\nL = 12\n$$\n\nNow substitute back to find $ C $:\n\n$$\nC = L + 11 = 12 + 11 = 23\n$$\n\n**Answer: 23**"
"We are given:\n\n- The **width** of the rectangle is **4 inches**.\n- The **perimeter** of the rectangle is **30 inches**.\n- We are to find the **area** of the rectangle.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the perimeter formula\n\nThe formula for the **perimeter** of a rectangle is:\n\n$$\nP = 2 \\times (\\text{length} + \\text{width})\n$$\n\nWe know:\n- $ P = 30 $\n- $ \\text{width} = 4 $\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n30 = 2 \\times (\\text{length} + 4)\n$$\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n$$\n15 = \\text{length} + 4\n$$\n\nSolve for **length**:\n\n$$\n\\text{length} = 15 - 4 = 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the area formula\n\nThe formula for the **area** of a rectangle is:\n\n$$\nA = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the known values:\n\n$$\nA = 11 \\times 4 = 44\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 44**"
"We are given the number of candies of different colors and asked to find how many **candies remained** after Carlos ate all the yellow ones.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Define the number of red candies\n\nWe are told:\n- There are **40 red candies**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Red} = 40\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of yellow candies\n\nWe are told:\n- There are **20 less than three times as many yellow candies as red candies**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Yellow} = 3 \\times \\text{Red} - 20 = 3 \\times 40 - 20 = 120 - 20 = 100\n$$\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Yellow} = 100\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the number of blue candies\n\nWe are told:\n- There are **half as many blue candies as yellow candies**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Blue} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{Yellow} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100 = 50\n$$\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Blue} = 50\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total number of candies\n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = \\text{Red} + \\text{Yellow} + \\text{Blue} = 40 + 100 + 50 = 190\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Subtract the yellow candies (since Carlos ate them all)\n\n$$\n\\text{Candies remaining} = \\text{Total} - \\text{Yellow} = 190 - 100 = 90\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 90**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate how many sandwiches Samson ate on **Monday**.\n\n- **Lunch**: 3 sandwiches  \n- **Dinner**: Twice as many as lunch \u2192 2 \u00d7 3 = 6 sandwiches  \n- **Breakfast the next morning (Tuesday)**: 1 sandwich  \n\nSo, **total for Monday** = 3 (lunch) + 6 (dinner) = **9 sandwiches**\n\n### Step 2: Calculate how many sandwiches Samson ate on **Tuesday**.\n\n- He only ate **1 sandwich** for breakfast.\n\nSo, **total for Tuesday** = 1 sandwich\n\n### Step 3: Find the difference between Monday and Tuesday.\n\n- Monday: 9 sandwiches  \n- Tuesday: 1 sandwich  \n- Difference = 9 \u2212 1 = **8**\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 8**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Carla can **cook a batch of waffles** in **10 minutes**.\n- Carla can **cook chicken-fried steak** in **6 minutes**.\n\nWe need to find how long it will take her to cook **3 steaks** and **1 batch of waffles**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Time to cook 3 steaks\n\nEach chicken-fried steak takes **6 minutes** to cook.  \nSo, for 3 steaks:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 6 = 18 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Time to cook 1 batch of waffles\n\n$$\n10 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total time\n\nAssuming Carla can cook the waffles and the steaks **simultaneously** (i.e., she can cook both at the same time), the total time will be the **maximum** of the two times.\n\n- Time for 3 steaks: **18 minutes**\n- Time for 1 batch of waffles: **10 minutes**\n\nSo, the total time is:\n\n$$\n\\max(18, 10) = 18 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 18**"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **First dog's food consumption:**  \n   The first dog eats **1.5 cups** of dog food per day.\n\n2. **Second dog's food consumption:**  \n   The second dog eats **twice as much** as the first dog.  \n   So,  \n   $ 2 \\times 1.5 = 3.0 $ cups per day.\n\n3. **Third dog's food consumption:**  \n   The third dog eats **2.5 cups more** than the second dog.  \n   So,  \n   $ 3.0 + 2.5 = 5.5 $ cups per day.\n\n4. **Total food needed per day:**  \n   Add the food for all three dogs:  \n   $ 1.5 + 3.0 + 5.5 = 10.0 $ cups.\n\n**Answer: 10.0**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Determine total number of appetizers needed**\n\nPatsy has **30 guests**, and she needs **6 appetizers per guest**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total appetizers needed} = 30 \\times 6 = 180\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Calculate the number of appetizers she is currently making**\n\nShe is making:\n\n- **3 dozen deviled eggs**  \n  $ 3 \\times 12 = 36 $ appetizers\n\n- **2 dozen pigs in a blanket**  \n  $ 2 \\times 12 = 24 $ appetizers\n\n- **2 dozen kebabs**  \n  $ 2 \\times 12 = 24 $ appetizers\n\n$$\n\\text{Total appetizers made} = 36 + 24 + 24 = 84\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Find how many more appetizers she needs**\n\n$$\n\\text{Appetizers needed} - \\text{Appetizers made} = 180 - 84 = 96\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Convert the remaining appetizers to dozens**\n\n$$\n\\text{Dozens needed} = \\frac{96}{12} = 8\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 8}}\n$$"
"Let's break this down step by step and count the number of legs in the room.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Count the legs of the furniture**\n\n#### **Tables:**\n- 4 tables with **4 legs each**  \n  \u2192 4 \u00d7 4 = **16 legs**\n\n- 3 tables with **3 legs each**  \n  \u2192 3 \u00d7 3 = **9 legs**\n\n- 1 table with **1 leg**  \n  \u2192 1 \u00d7 1 = **1 leg**\n\n**Total legs from tables: 16 + 9 + 1 = 26 legs**\n\n---\n\n#### **Sofa:**\n- 1 sofa with **4 legs**  \n  \u2192 4 legs\n\n---\n\n#### **Chairs:**\n- 2 chairs with **4 legs each**  \n  \u2192 2 \u00d7 4 = **8 legs**\n\n- 1 rocking chair with **2 legs**  \n  \u2192 2 legs\n\n**Total legs from chairs: 8 + 2 = 10 legs**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Add all the legs together**\n\n- Tables: 26 legs  \n- Sofa: 4 legs  \n- Chairs: 10 legs  \n\n**Total legs in the room: 26 + 4 + 10 = 40 legs**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 40**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the prices of the drinks\n- **Cappuccino** = $2  \n- **Iced tea** = $3  \n- **Cafe latte** = $1.50  \n- **Espresso** = $1  \n\n### Step 2: Calculate the total cost of the drinks Sandy ordered\nSandy orders:\n- 3 cappuccinos: $ 3 \\times 2 = 6 $  \n- 2 iced teas: $ 2 \\times 3 = 6 $  \n- 2 cafe lattes: $ 2 \\times 1.5 = 3 $  \n- 2 espressos: $ 2 \\times 1 = 2 $  \n\nNow, add them all together:\n$$\n6 + 6 + 3 + 2 = 17\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the change from a $20 bill\n$$\n20 - 17 = 3\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 3**"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Weekly Income**\nAlex earns **$500** per week.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Tax Deduction**\n10% of his weekly income is deducted as tax.\n\n$$\n\\text{Tax} = 10\\% \\times 500 = 0.10 \\times 500 = 50\n$$\n\nSo, **$50** is taken out for tax.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Water Bill**\nHe pays **$55** for his water bill.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Tithe**\nHe gives away **10%** of his weekly income as a tithe.\n\n$$\n\\text{Tithe} = 10\\% \\times 500 = 0.10 \\times 500 = 50\n$$\n\nSo, **$50** is given as a tithe.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Total Deductions**\nAdd up all the deductions:\n\n- Tax: $50  \n- Water bill: $55  \n- Tithe: $50  \n\n$$\n\\text{Total deductions} = 50 + 55 + 50 = 155\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 6: Money Left**\nSubtract total deductions from his weekly income:\n\n$$\n\\text{Money left} = 500 - 155 = 345\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 345**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Understand the capacity of the feeder**\n\n- The bird feeder holds **2 cups** of birdseed.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: How much birdseed is stolen by the squirrel**\n\n- The squirrel steals **0.5 cups** of birdseed **every week**.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: How much birdseed is actually available for the birds**\n\n- Total birdseed in the feeder: **2 cups**\n- Squirrel steals: **0.5 cups**\n- So, **available for birds** = 2 - 0.5 = **1.5 cups**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: How many birds can be fed with 1.5 cups**\n\n- Each **cup** of birdseed can feed **14 birds**\n- So, 1.5 cups can feed:  \n  $ 1.5 \\times 14 = 21 $ birds\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\nIvan\u2019s bird feeder feeds **21 birds** weekly.\n\n**Answer: 21**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- **Total students**: 25  \n- **Vampire-themed requests**: 11  \n- **Pumpkin-themed requests**: 14  \n\nThe teacher needs to buy **11 vampire-themed bags** and **14 pumpkin-themed bags**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the pricing options\n\n- **Pack of 5 bags of each theme** costs **$3**.\n- **Individual bag** of each theme costs **$1**.\n\nWe want to **minimize the total cost** for 11 vampire and 14 pumpkin bags.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Strategy\n\nWe should **buy as many packs of 5 as possible** for each theme, since packs are cheaper per bag than individual bags.\n\nLet\u2019s handle each theme separately.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Vampire-themed bags (11 total)\n\n- How many full packs of 5 can we buy?  \n  $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 2 $ packs \u2192 10 bags  \n  Remaining: $ 11 - 10 = 1 $ bag\n\n- Cost for 2 packs: $ 2 \\times 3 = 6 $  \n- Cost for 1 individual: $ 1 \\times 1 = 1 $  \n- **Total for vampire bags**: $ 6 + 1 = 7 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Pumpkin-themed bags (14 total)\n\n- How many full packs of 5 can we buy?  \n  $ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{14}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 2 $ packs \u2192 10 bags  \n  Remaining: $ 14 - 10 = 4 $ bags\n\n- Cost for 2 packs: $ 2 \\times 3 = 6 $  \n- Cost for 4 individual: $ 4 \\"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The zoo has **16 pandas**.\n- The pandas are **paired into mates** (so there are 8 couples).\n- **Only 25% of the panda couples get pregnant after mating.**\n- Each pregnant couple has **one baby**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the number of panda couples\n\nThere are 16 pandas, and they are paired into mates. So:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of couples} = \\frac{16}{2} = 8\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of couples that get pregnant\n\n25% of the 8 couples get pregnant:\n\n$$\n0.25 \\times 8 = 2\n$$\n\nSo, **2 couples** get pregnant.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the number of babies born\n\nEach pregnant couple has **1 baby**, so:\n\n$$\n2 \\text{ couples} \\times 1 \\text{ baby per couple} = 2 \\text{ babies}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 2**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The movie theater has **6 screens**.\n- Each **screen** shows **movies back-to-back** all day.\n- The theater is open for **8 hours**.\n- Each **movie** lasts **2 hours**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many movies can be shown on one screen in 8 hours.\n\nEach movie is 2 hours long, so in 8 hours, the number of movies that can be shown on one screen is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8 \\text{ hours}}{2 \\text{ hours per movie}} = 4 \\text{ movies}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Multiply by the number of screens.\n\nSince there are 6 screens, and each screen shows 4 movies, the total number of movies shown in the theater is:\n\n$$\n6 \\text{ screens} \\times 4 \\text{ movies per screen} = 24 \\text{ movies}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 24**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Original price of the porcelain vase: **$200**\n- Discount: **25% off**\n- Sales tax: **10%**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the discount amount\n\n$$\n\\text{Discount} = 25\\% \\times 200 = 0.25 \\times 200 = 50\n$$\n\nSo, the discounted price is:\n\n$$\n200 - 50 = 150\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the sales tax\n\n$$\n\\text{Sales tax} = 10\\% \\times 150 = 0.10 \\times 150 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add the sales tax to the discounted price\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 150 + 15 = 165\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 165**"
"We are told:\n\n1. **Yuan is 7 years older than his brother David.**\n2. **Yuan is also twice David's age.**\n\nLet\u2019s define the variables:\n\n- Let $ D $ be David's age.\n- Then Yuan's age is $ D + 7 $ (from the first statement).\n- Also, from the second statement, Yuan's age is $ 2D $ (twice David's age).\n\nSo, we can set up the equation:\n\n$$\nD + 7 = 2D\n$$\n\nNow, solve for $ D $:\n\n$$\nD + 7 = 2D \\\\\n7 = 2D - D \\\\\n7 = D\n$$\n\n**Answer: 7**"
"Let's break this problem into steps and solve it carefully.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Find the number of students in the restroom**\n\nWe are told:\n\n> \"2 students are in the restroom.\"\n\nSo, **students in the restroom = 2**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Find the number of students absent**\n\nWe are told:\n\n> \"One less than three times that number of students are absent.\"\n\nLet\u2019s interpret this:\n\n- \"That number\" refers to the number of students in the restroom, which is **2**.\n- \"Three times that number\" = 3 \u00d7 2 = 6\n- \"One less than that\" = 6 \u2212 1 = **5**\n\nSo, **students absent = 5**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Find the number of students in the classroom**\n\nWe are told:\n\n> \"There are four rows with six desks each that are all 2/3 full.\"\n\nLet\u2019s calculate the total number of desks:\n\n- 4 rows \u00d7 6 desks per row = **24 desks**\n\nEach desk is **2/3 full**, so the number of students in the classroom is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3} \\times 24 = 16\n$$\n\nSo, **students in the classroom = 16**\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Total number of students Carla teaches**\n\nWe need to include:\n\n- Students in the classroom: 16\n- Students in the restroom: 2\n- Students absent: 5\n\nSo, total number of students:\n\n$$\n16 + 2 + 5 = 23\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{23}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 23**"
"Step 1: Find the total miles Clover walks in one day.  \nHe walks 1.5 miles in the morning and 1.5 miles in the evening.  \nSo, total per day = 1.5 + 1.5 = **3 miles**.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the total miles walked in 30 days.  \nTotal = 3 miles/day \u00d7 30 days = **90 miles**.\n\nAnswer: 90"
"Step 1: Tyler starts with 21 CDs.  \nStep 2: He gives away a third of his CDs.  \nA third of 21 is:  \n$$\n\\frac{21}{3} = 7\n$$  \nSo he gives away 7 CDs.  \nNow he has:  \n$$\n21 - 7 = 14 \\text{ CDs}\n$$  \n\nStep 3: He buys 8 new CDs.  \nNow he has:  \n$$\n14 + 8 = 22 \\text{ CDs}\n$$  \n\n**Answer: 22**"
"Let's solve this step by step:\n\n1. **Initial stock**: Steve has 200 fish.\n\n2. **Sells 50 fish**:\n   $$\n   200 - 50 = 150 \\text{ fish remaining}\n   $$\n\n3. **A third of the remaining fish becomes spoiled**:\n   $$\n   \\frac{1}{3} \\times 150 = 50 \\text{ fish spoiled}\n   $$\n   So, the number of fish that are **not spoiled** is:\n   $$\n   150 - 50 = 100 \\text{ fish}\n   $$\n\n4. **New stock arrives**: A new stock of 200 more fish arrives.\n   $$\n   100 + 200 = 300 \\text{ fish}\n   $$\n\n**Final answer**:  \nSteve now has 300 fish in stock.\n\nAnswer: 300"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\nWe are given the following:\n\n- **Tabitha** caught **22** pieces of candy.\n- **Stan** caught **13** pieces of candy.\n- **Julie** caught **half the amount** of candy as Tabitha.\n- **Carlos** caught **twice as much** candy as Stan.\n\n### Step 1: Find how much Julie caught\nJulie caught half the amount of candy as Tabitha:\n\n$$\n\\text{Julie's candy} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 22 = 11\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Find how much Carlos caught\nCarlos caught twice as much as Stan:\n\n$$\n\\text{Carlos's candy} = 2 \\times 13 = 26\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Add up all the candy\nNow, add the candy caught by all the friends:\n\n- Tabitha: 22\n- Stan: 13\n- Julie: 11\n- Carlos: 26\n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = 22 + 13 + 11 + 26 = 72\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 72**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the normal doctor's cost\n- A normal doctor charges **$200 per visit**.\n- Tom goes to the **discount clinic**, which is **70% cheaper**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the cost at the discount clinic\n\nIf the clinic is **70% cheaper**, that means the cost is **30% of the normal price** (since 100% - 70% = 30%).\n\n$$\n\\text{Discounted price per visit} = 200 \\times 0.30 = 60 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine how many visits Tom makes\n\n- A **normal doctor** requires **1 visit**.\n- The **discount clinic** requires **2 visits**.\n\nSo, Tom pays for **2 visits** at the discount clinic.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost at discount clinic} = 2 \\times 60 = 120 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the savings\n\n- If Tom had gone to the normal doctor, he would have paid **$200** for **1 visit**.\n- But he went to the discount clinic and paid **$120** for **2 visits**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Savings} = 200 - 120 = 80 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{80}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 80**"
"Let's break down Sean's weekly spending step by step.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Almond Croissant and Salami & Cheese Croissant**\n- Each croissant costs $4.50\n- He gets **1 almond croissant** and **1 salami & cheese croissant**\n- Total for these two:  \n  $ 4.50 + 4.50 = 9.00 $\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Plain Croissant**\n- Costs $3.00\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Loaf of Focaccia**\n- Costs $4.00\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Lattes**\n- He buys **2 lattes** at $2.50 each\n- Total for lattes:  \n  $ 2.50 \u00d7 2 = 5.00 $\n\n---\n\n### Now, add all the costs together:\n\n- Almond & Salami Croissants: $9.00  \n- Plain Croissant: $3.00  \n- Focaccia: $4.00  \n- Lattes: $5.00  \n\n**Total cost = 9.00 + 3.00 + 4.00 + 5.00 = 21.00**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 21.00**"
"Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Bingley starts with 5 bracelets.**\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Kelly gives Bingley a fourth of her 16 bracelets.**\n\n- A fourth of 16 is:  \n  $ \\frac{1}{4} \\times 16 = 4 $\n\n- So, Bingley receives 4 more bracelets.\n\n- Now Bingley has:  \n  $ 5 + 4 = 9 $ bracelets.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Bingley gives a third of his bracelets to his little sister.**\n\n- Bingley has 9 bracelets.  \n- A third of 9 is:  \n  $ \\frac{1}{3} \\times 9 = 3 $\n\n- He gives 3 bracelets to his sister.\n\n- So, Bingley has:  \n  $ 9 - 3 = 6 $ bracelets left.\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer: 6**  \nAnswer: 6"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Understand the problem\n- Matt needs to buy plates for his home.\n- He only wants to do dishes **once a week**.\n- **Three days a week**, it's only **Matt and his son** eating. They each use **1 plate**.\n- On the **remaining days**, **his parents join them**, and **everyone uses 2 plates** that day.\n- We need to find out how many **plates he needs to buy**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the number of days in each category\n\n- Total days in a week = 7\n- 3 days: Matt and son (1 plate each)\n- Remaining days = 7 - 3 = **4 days**\n- On these 4 days, **Matt, his son, and both parents** are eating. That's 4 people.\n- Each person uses **2 plates** that day.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the number of plates used each day\n\n- **3 days**:\n  - 2 people \u00d7 1 plate = **2 plates per day**\n  - Total for 3 days = 3 \u00d7 2 = **6 plates**\n\n- **4 days**:\n  - 4 people \u00d7 2 plates = **8 plates per day**\n  - Total for 4 days = 4 \u00d7 8 = **32 plates**\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total plates needed per week\n\n- 6 (from 3 days) + 32 (from 4 days) = **38 plates**\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\nMatt needs to buy **38 plates**.\n\n**Answer: 38**"
"Let's break this down step by step to find out how much Maddie's mom spends on coffee per week.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: How much coffee does Maddie's mom use per week?**\n\n- Maddie makes **2 cups of coffee per day**.\n- Each cup uses **1.5 ounces of coffee beans**.\n- So, per day:  \n  $ 2 \\text{ cups/day} \\times 1.5 \\text{ ounces/cup} = 3 \\text{ ounces/day} $\n\n- Per week (7 days):  \n  $ 3 \\text{ ounces/day} \\times 7 \\text{ days} = 21 \\text{ ounces/week} $\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: How much does the coffee cost per week?**\n\n- A **bag of coffee** costs **$8** and contains **10.5 ounces** of beans.\n- So, the cost per ounce of coffee is:  \n  $ \\frac{8}{10.5} \\approx 0.7619 \\text{ dollars/ounce} $\n\n- Total cost for 21 ounces:  \n  $ 21 \\times 0.7619 \\approx 16.00 \\text{ dollars} $\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: How much does the milk cost per week?**\n\n- Maddie uses **\u00bd gallon of milk per week**.\n- A **gallon of milk** costs **$4**, so:\n  $ \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 = 2 \\text{ dollars/week} $\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Total weekly cost**\n\n- Coffee: $16.00\n- Milk: $2.00\n\n**Total cost per week:**\n$$\n16.00 + 2.00 = 18.00\n$$\n\n---\n\n**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Clean the house**: 7 tasks  \n- **Take a shower**: 1 task  \n- **Make dinner**: 4 tasks  \n\nTotal number of tasks = 7 + 1 + 4 = **12 tasks**\n\nEach task takes **10 minutes**.\n\n### Step 1: Total time in minutes\n$$\n12 \\text{ tasks} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes per task} = 120 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Convert minutes to hours\n$$\n120 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{120}{60} = 2 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 2**"
"We are given that James collects **500 tins in a week**. Let's break down the number of tins he collects each day and then find out how many he collects on the remaining days.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Tins collected on the first day\n- **Day 1:** 50 tins\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Tins collected on the second day\n- He collects **3 times** the number from the first day.\n- So, **Day 2:**  \n  $ 3 \\times 50 = 150 $ tins\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Tins collected on the third day\n- He collects **50 tins fewer** than the second day.\n- So, **Day 3:**  \n  $ 150 - 50 = 100 $ tins\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total tins collected in the first 3 days\n- **Day 1 + Day 2 + Day 3**  \n  $ 50 + 150 + 100 = 300 $ tins\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Tins left to collect for the remaining days\n- Total tins needed: 500  \n- Tins already collected: 300  \n- So, **tins left for the remaining 4 days** (since a week has 7 days, and 3 days are already accounted for):  \n  $ 500 - 300 = 200 $ tins\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Tins collected per day for the remaining 4 days\n- He collects the **same number of tins each day** for the remaining 4 days.\n- So, **tins per day** =  \n  $ \\frac{200}{4} = 50 $ tins per day\n\n---\n\n###"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Total cost of the meal**\n\n- James orders a **steak and egg meal** for **$16**.\n- His friend orders **chicken fried steak** for **$14**.\n\nSo, the **total cost of the meal** is:\n\n$$\n16 + 14 = 30 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Tip amount**\n\nThey tip **20%** of the total bill.\n\n$$\n\\text{Tip} = 20\\% \\times 30 = 0.2 \\times 30 = 6 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\nSo, the **total bill including tip** is:\n\n$$\n30 + 6 = 36 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Split the bill**\n\nJames and his friend split the **bill before tip** (i.e., the $30), and then James pays the **tip** as well.\n\n- Each pays **half of the $30**:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{2} = 15 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\nSo, **James pays $15** for his half of the bill.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: James also pays the tip**\n\nHe pays **$6** in tip.\n\nSo, the **total amount James pays** is:\n\n$$\n15 + 6 = 21 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{21}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 21**"
"Let's solve the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: How many pickles can Sammy eat?**\n\nSammy can eat **15** pickle slices.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: How many pickles can Tammy eat?**\n\nTammy can eat **twice as much as Sammy**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Tammy} = 2 \\times 15 = 30 \\text{ pickle slices}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: How many pickles does Ron eat?**\n\nRon eats **20% fewer** pickle slices than Tammy.\n\nTo find 20% of Tammy's amount:\n\n$$\n20\\% \\text{ of } 30 = 0.2 \\times 30 = 6\n$$\n\nSo, Ron eats:\n\n$$\n30 - 6 = 24 \\text{ pickle slices}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{24}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 24**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Cupcakes are sold in **packages of 10** and **15**.\n- Jean **bought 4 packs of 15** cupcakes.\n- She wants to give **1 cupcake to each of 100 children**.\n- We need to find how many **packs of 10** she should buy to have enough cupcakes for all 100 children.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the number of cupcakes from the 4 packs of 15\n\nEach pack of 15 has 15 cupcakes, so:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 15 = 60 \\text{ cupcakes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many more cupcakes are needed\n\nShe needs 100 cupcakes, and she already has 60, so:\n\n$$\n100 - 60 = 40 \\text{ more cupcakes needed}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine how many packs of 10 are needed for the remaining 40 cupcakes\n\nEach pack of 10 has 10 cupcakes, so:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{10} = 4 \\text{ packs of 10}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{4}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 4**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Initially, there are **74 apples** in the basket.\n- **Ricki removes 14 apples**.\n- **Samson removes twice as many as Ricki**, so Samson removes $ 2 \\times 14 = 28 $ apples.\n\nNow, let's calculate the total number of apples removed:\n\n$$\n14 + 28 = 42\n$$\n\nNow, subtract the total number of apples removed from the initial number:\n\n$$\n74 - 42 = 32\n$$\n\n**Answer: 32**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The school has **15 classrooms**.\n- **One-third** of these classrooms have **30 desks** each.\n- The **remaining two-thirds** have **25 desks** each.\n- Only **one student can sit at one desk**.\n- We are to find how many **students** the school can **accommodate** so that **everyone has their own desk**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find how many classrooms have 30 desks\n\nOne-third of 15 classrooms is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} \\times 15 = 5 \\text{ classrooms}\n$$\n\nSo, **5 classrooms** have **30 desks** each.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many classrooms have 25 desks\n\nThe rest of the classrooms is:\n\n$$\n15 - 5 = 10 \\text{ classrooms}\n$$\n\nSo, **10 classrooms** have **25 desks** each.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate total number of desks\n\n- 5 classrooms \u00d7 30 desks = 150 desks\n- 10 classrooms \u00d7 25 desks = 250 desks\n\nTotal desks = 150 + 250 = **400 desks**\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Determine how many students can be accommodated\n\nSince **only one student can sit at one desk**, the total number of students the school can accommodate is equal to the total number of desks.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{400}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 400**"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **Price of the bed frame**: $75  \n2. **Price of the bed**: 10 times the price of the bed frame  \n   $$\n   10 \\times 75 = 750\n   $$\n3. **Total price before discount**:  \n   $$\n   75 + 750 = 825\n   $$\n4. **Discount**: 20% off  \n   $$\n   20\\% \\text{ of } 825 = 0.20 \\times 825 = 165\n   $$\n5. **Final price after discount**:  \n   $$\n   825 - 165 = 660\n   $$\n\n**Answer: 660**"
"Step 1: James takes 20 units per semester.  \nStep 2: Each unit costs $50.  \nStep 3: Cost per semester = 20 units \u00d7 $50/unit = $1000.  \nStep 4: For 2 semesters, total cost = $1000/semester \u00d7 2 semesters = $2000.  \n\nAnswer: 2000"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Each **row** has **6 chairs**.\n- There are **20 rows**.\n- Each **chair** holds **5 people**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the total number of chairs in the church.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total chairs} = \\text{Chairs per row} \\times \\text{Number of rows} = 6 \\times 20 = 120\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the total number of people that can sit in the chairs.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total people} = \\text{Total chairs} \\times \\text{People per chair} = 120 \\times 5 = 600\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 600}}\n$$"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **First 2 days**: Tim sleeps 6 hours each day.  \n   So, total sleep for 2 days = $ 6 \\, \\text{hours/day} \\times 2 \\, \\text{days} = 12 \\, \\text{hours} $.\n\n2. **Next 2 days**: Tim sleeps 10 hours each day.  \n   So, total sleep for the next 2 days = $ 10 \\, \\text{hours/day} \\times 2 \\, \\text{days} = 20 \\, \\text{hours} $.\n\n3. **Total sleep** = Sleep from first 2 days + Sleep from next 2 days  \n   = $ 12 \\, \\text{hours} + 20 \\, \\text{hours} = 32 \\, \\text{hours} $.\n\n**Answer: 32**."
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the total number of tickets needed\nJeanne wants to ride three rides:\n- **Ferris wheel**: 5 tickets  \n- **Roller coaster**: 4 tickets  \n- **Bumper cars**: 4 tickets  \n\nTotal tickets needed = 5 + 4 + 4 = **13 tickets**\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many tickets Jeanne already has\nJeanne has **5 tickets**.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate how many more tickets she needs\nMore tickets needed = Total tickets needed \u2013 Tickets she has  \n= 13 \u2013 5 = **8 tickets**\n\n### Final Answer:\nJeanne should buy **8 more tickets**.\n\n**Answer: 8**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **40%** of the students have elected to learn from home.\n- The **remaining 60%** are divided into **two equal groups**, and **only one of those groups is physically in school on any day**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the percentage of students not at home\n\n- 40% are at home, so the remaining percentage is:\n  $$\n  100\\% - 40\\% = 60\\%\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Divide the remaining 60% into two equal groups\n\n- The 60% is split into **two equal groups**, so each group is:\n  $$\n  \\frac{60\\%}{2} = 30\\%\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the percentage of students present in school\n\n- Only **one of the two groups** is physically in school on any given day, so:\n  $$\n  30\\% \\text{ of the students are present in school}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{30}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 30**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Steve has **12** popsicle sticks.\n- Sid has **twice** as many as Steve.\n- Sam has **thrice** as many as Sid.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find how many popsicle sticks Sid has.\n\nSid has **twice** as many as Steve:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sid} = 2 \\times \\text{Steve} = 2 \\times 12 = 24\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many popsicle sticks Sam has.\n\nSam has **thrice** as many as Sid:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sam} = 3 \\times \\text{Sid} = 3 \\times 24 = 72\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add up the total number of popsicle sticks.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = \\text{Steve} + \\text{Sid} + \\text{Sam} = 12 + 24 + 72 = 108\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 108**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- John buys **1000 balloons**.\n- Each balloon holds **10 liters of air**.\n- He buys **500-liter tanks of gas**.\n\nWe need to find out how many **500-liter tanks** he needs to fill **all the balloons**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total amount of air needed\n\nEach balloon holds 10 liters, and there are 1000 balloons:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total air needed} = 1000 \\times 10 = 10,000 \\text{ liters}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many 500-liter tanks are needed\n\nEach tank contains 500 liters, so the number of tanks required is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000}{500} = 20\n$$\n\nSince 20 tanks provide exactly 10,000 liters, John needs **20 tanks**.\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 20**"
"We are told that:\n\n- Nadia walked **18 kilometers**.\n- Nadia's distance is **twice** the distance Hannah walked.\n\n### Step 1: Let the distance Hannah walked be $ x $ kilometers.\n\nThen, according to the problem:\n$$\n2x = 18\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Solve for $ x $:\n$$\nx = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n$$\n\nSo, Hannah walked **9 kilometers**.\n\n### Step 3: Add the distances walked by both girls:\n$$\n\\text{Total distance} = 18 + 9 = 27\n$$\n\n**Answer: 27**"
"Eric has a total of 20 marbles.  \nHe has 12 white marbles and 6 blue marbles.  \n\nTo find the number of green marbles, subtract the number of white and blue marbles from the total:\n\n$$\n\\text{Green marbles} = 20 - (12 + 6) = 20 - 18 = 2\n$$\n\n**Answer: 2**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Initially, there are **100 cars** in the **front parking lot**.\n- There are **two times more vehicles** at the **back parking lot** than in the **front**.\n- The **total number of cars** at the **end of the play** is **700**.\n- We are to find how many **more cars packed into the parking lot** during the play.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of cars in the back parking lot.\n\nIt says: \"She saw two times more vehicles at the back than there were in the front parking lot.\"\n\nThis means:\n$$\n\\text{Back parking lot cars} = 2 \\times \\text{Front parking lot cars} = 2 \\times 100 = 200\n$$\n\nSo, initially, there are:\n- 100 cars in the front\n- 200 cars in the back\n\nTotal initial cars:\n$$\n100 + 200 = 300\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total number of cars at the end of the play is 700.\n\nSo, the number of **additional cars** that arrived during the play is:\n$$\n700 - 300 = 400\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{400}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 400**"
"We are given:\n\n- The solver flipped a coin **211 times**.\n- He got **65 heads**.\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of tails\nTotal flips = 211  \nNumber of heads = 65  \nSo, number of tails = Total flips - Number of heads  \n$$\n\\text{Tails} = 211 - 65 = 146\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Find how many more tails than heads\n$$\n\\text{Difference} = \\text{Tails} - \\text{Heads} = 146 - 65 = 81\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{81}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 81**"
"We are told that **Mariel is a dog walker**, and while walking her pack of dogs, she gets **tangled up in the leashes of another dog walker and their 3 dogs**. There are **36 legs** tangled up in leashes.\n\nWe are to find: **How many dogs is Mariel walking?**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Identify the people and animals involved\n\n- **Mariel** (a human) is walking dogs.\n- There is **another dog walker** (another human).\n- That other walker has **3 dogs**.\n- So, the total number of **people** involved is:  \n  - Mariel (1)  \n  - Other dog walker (1)  \n  \u2192 Total people = 2\n\n- The **dogs** involved are:  \n  - The 3 dogs from the other walker  \n  - The dogs that Mariel is walking (let\u2019s call this number **x**)\n\nSo, total number of **dogs** = 3 (from other walker) + x (Mariel\u2019s dogs) = 3 + x\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Count the legs\n\nEach **human** has **2 legs**.  \nEach **dog** has **4 legs**.\n\nTotal legs = legs from humans + legs from dogs\n\nLet\u2019s compute:\n\n- Humans: 2 people \u2192 2 \u00d7 2 = 4 legs\n- Dogs: (3 + x) dogs \u2192 (3 + x) \u00d7 4 = 12 + 4x legs\n\nTotal legs = 4 + 12 + 4x = 16 + 4x\n\nWe are told that the total number of legs tangled up is **36**.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n16 + 4x = 36\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Solve the equation\n\n$$\n16 + 4x = 36  \n\\Rightarrow 4x"
"Let's solve this step by step.\n\nWe are given:\n\n- A boat carrying:\n  - 20 **sheep**\n  - 10 **cows**\n  - 14 **dogs**\n\n**3 of the sheep drowned.**\n\n**Twice as many cows drowned as did sheep.**\n\n**All of the dogs made it to shore.**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many sheep drowned.\n\nWe are told:\n> 3 of the sheep drowned.\n\nSo, **3 sheep drowned**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many cows drowned.\n\nWe are told:\n> Twice as many cows drowned as did sheep.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Cows drowned} = 2 \\times \\text{Sheep drowned} = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n$$\n\nSo, **6 cows drowned**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine how many animals made it to shore.\n\nWe know:\n- Total sheep: 20 \u2192 3 drowned \u2192 **20 - 3 = 17 sheep survived**\n- Total cows: 10 \u2192 6 drowned \u2192 **10 - 6 = 4 cows survived**\n- Total dogs: 14 \u2192 **All 14 dogs survived**\n\nNow, add the number of animals that survived:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total animals that made it to shore} = 17 \\text{ (sheep)} + 4 \\text{ (cows)} + 14 \\text{ (dogs)} = 35\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 35**"
"We are given the number of tickets sold for each group and the estimated number of omelets each group will eat. We also know that the club wants to make **25 extra omelets** and that **2 eggs are needed per omelet**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the number of omelets needed for each group\n\n- **Small children**: 53 tickets \u00d7 0.5 omelet = 26.5 omelets  \n- **Older children**: 35 tickets \u00d7 1 omelet = 35 omelets  \n- **Adults**: 75 tickets \u00d7 2 omelets = 150 omelets  \n- **Seniors**: 37 tickets \u00d7 1.5 omelets = 55.5 omelets  \n\nNow, add these up:\n\n$$\n26.5 + 35 + 150 + 55.5 = 267 \\text{ omelets}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add the extra omelets\n\n$$\n267 + 25 = 292 \\text{ omelets}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the number of eggs needed\n\n$$\n292 \\text{ omelets} \u00d7 2 \\text{ eggs per omelet} = 584 \\text{ eggs}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{584}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 584**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- There are **4 puppies** and **3 dogs** at the camp.\n- Each **dog eats twice as much food as a puppy**.\n- Each **puppy eats three times as often as a dog**.\n- A **dog eats 4 pounds of food three times a day**.\n\nWe are to find the **total amount of food** the **dogs and puppies** eat in **a day**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how much a dog eats in a day\n\nA dog eats **4 pounds of food** **three times a day**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Dog's daily food} = 4 \\times 3 = 12 \\text{ pounds}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how much a puppy eats in a day\n\nWe are told that **each dog eats twice as much food as a puppy**, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Puppy's food per meal} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2 \\text{ pounds per meal}\n$$\n\nAlso, **each puppy eats three times as often as a dog**. A dog eats 3 times a day, so a puppy eats:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 9 \\text{ times a day}\n$$\n\nSo, the **daily food for a puppy** is:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 9 = 18 \\text{ pounds}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate total food for all dogs and puppies\n\n- **3 dogs** eat:\n  $$\n  3 \\times 12 = 36 \\text{ pounds}\n  $$\n\n- **4 puppies** eat:\n  $$\n  4 \\times 18 = 72 \\text{ pounds}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Add the totals\n\n$$\n\\text{Total food per day} = 36 + 72 ="
"We are given that Heloise has dogs and cats in the ratio of **10:17**, and the total number of pets is **189**.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Let the number of dogs = 10x, and the number of cats = 17x**\n\nSo, the total number of pets is:\n\n$$\n10x + 17x = 27x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the total number of pets is 189, so:\n\n$$\n27x = 189\n$$\n\nSolve for **x**:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{189}{27} = 7\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Find the number of dogs**\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of dogs} = 10x = 10 \\times 7 = 70\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: She gives 10 dogs to her friend Janet**\n\n$$\n\\text{Dogs remaining} = 70 - 10 = 60\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{60}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 60**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Antonio's recipe calls for **1/8 of a pound of hamburger per meatball**.\n- Antonio has **8 family members**, including himself.\n- He uses **4 pounds of hamburger** to make meatballs.\n- Each family member eats the **same number of meatballs**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the total number of meatballs\n\nEach meatball uses **1/8 pound** of hamburger.\n\nSo, the total number of meatballs is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total meatballs} = \\frac{4 \\text{ pounds}}{1/8 \\text{ pound per meatball}} = 4 \\div \\frac{1}{8} = 4 \\times 8 = 32\n$$\n\nSo, there are **32 meatballs** in total.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Divide the meatballs equally among 8 family members\n\n$$\n\\text{Meatballs per person} = \\frac{32}{8} = 4\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\nAntonio will eat **4 meatballs**.\n\n**Answer: 4**"
"Let's solve this step by step:\n\n1. **Randy starts with $30.**  \n   - Initial amount: $30\n\n2. **He spends $10 on lunch.**  \n   - Money left: $30 - $10 = **$20**\n\n3. **He spends a quarter of the money he had left on an ice cream cone.**  \n   - A quarter of $20 is: $ \\frac{1}{4} \\times 20 = 5 $  \n   - Money left after ice cream: $20 - $5 = **$15**\n\n**Answer: 15**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- In **5 years**, Andy will be **twice as old as Rahim is now**.\n- Rahim is **6 years old now**.\n- We are to find how much **older Andy is than Rahim**, **right now**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let\u2019s define variables.\n\nLet:\n- $ A $ = Andy's current age\n- $ R $ = Rahim's current age = 6 (given)\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the information about the future.\n\nIn **5 years**, Andy's age will be:\n$$\nA + 5\n$$\n\nAt that time, Andy will be **twice as old as Rahim is now**:\n$$\nA + 5 = 2 \\times R = 2 \\times 6 = 12\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Solve for Andy's current age.\n\n$$\nA + 5 = 12 \\\\\nA = 12 - 5 = 7\n$$\n\nSo, Andy is **7 years old now**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Find the difference in their ages.\n\n$$\n\\text{Difference} = A - R = 7 - 6 = 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 1**"
"We are told:\n\n- The show has **5 actors at a time**.\n- Each actor is in the show for **15 minutes**.\n- Then **another 5 actors** come in.\n- The show runs for **1 hour = 60 minutes**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: How many 15-minute periods are in 1 hour?\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{15} = 4 \\text{ periods}\n$$\n\nSo, the show has **4 rounds** of 15 minutes each.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: How many actors are needed for each 15-minute period?\n\nEach period has **5 actors**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total number of actors needed for the entire show\n\n$$\n4 \\text{ periods} \\times 5 \\text{ actors per period} = 20 \\text{ actors}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 20}}\n$$"
"We are given the following:\n\n- **Old refrigerator cost:** $0.85 per day  \n- **New refrigerator cost:** $0.45 per day  \n- **Number of days in a month:** 30 days  \n\n### Step 1: Find the daily savings\n$$\n\\text{Daily savings} = 0.85 - 0.45 = 0.40 \\text{ dollars per day}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Find the total savings for 30 days\n$$\n\\text{Total savings} = 0.40 \\times 30 = 12.00 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{12}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 12**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Cost of corn seeds**: $50  \n- **Cost of fertilizers and pesticides**: $35  \n- **Cost of labor**: $15  \n- **Total cost** = $50 + $35 + $15 = **$100**\n\nHe harvested **10 bags of corn**.\n\nHe wants to make a **10% profit** on the total cost.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total cost\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 50 + 35 + 15 = 100\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the desired total revenue (including 10% profit)\n\nA 10% profit on the total cost means:\n$$\n\\text{Profit} = 10\\% \\times 100 = 10\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Total revenue} = \\text{Total cost} + \\text{Profit} = 100 + 10 = 110\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the price per bag\n\n$$\n\\text{Price per bag} = \\frac{\\text{Total revenue}}{\\text{Number of bags}} = \\frac{110}{10} = 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 11**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- On average, **6 cows** produce **108 liters** of milk per week.\n- Over **5 weeks**, the cows produced **2160 liters** of milk.\n- We are to find the **total number of cows** on the farm.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the milk production per cow per week\n\nFrom the given:\n- 6 cows produce 108 liters per week.\n\nSo, the milk production per cow per week is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{108 \\text{ liters}}{6 \\text{ cows}} = 18 \\text{ liters per cow per week}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the total milk production per cow over 5 weeks\n\n$$\n18 \\text{ liters/week} \\times 5 \\text{ weeks} = 90 \\text{ liters per cow over 5 weeks}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the total number of cows\n\nWe know that the total milk produced over 5 weeks is 2160 liters. If each cow produces 90 liters over 5 weeks, then the number of cows is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2160 \\text{ liters}}{90 \\text{ liters per cow}} = 24 \\text{ cows}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 24**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The hot air balloon has **200 balloons**.\n- After **half an hour**, **1/5 of the total number of balloons** have blown up.\n- After **another hour**, **twice the number of balloons that had already blown up** also blow up.\n- We are to find how many **balloons remain intact**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total number of balloons\n\n$$\n\\text{Total balloons} = 200\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Balloons that blow up after half an hour\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5} \\times 200 = 40 \\text{ balloons}\n$$\n\nSo, **40 balloons** have blown up after half an hour.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Balloons that blow up after another hour\n\nIt says: **\"twice the number of balloons that had already blown up\"**.\n\nThat is:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 40 = 80 \\text{ balloons}\n$$\n\nSo, **80 more balloons** blow up after the second hour.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total number of balloons that have blown up\n\n$$\n40 + 80 = 120 \\text{ balloons}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Number of balloons that remain intact\n\n$$\n200 - 120 = 80\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{80}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 80**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Viviana has five more chocolate chips than Susana.**\n- **Susana has 3/4 as many vanilla chips as Viviana.**\n- **Viviana has 20 vanilla chips.**\n- **Susana has 25 chocolate chips.**\n\nWe are to find the **total number of chips** that Viviana and Susana have together.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of chocolate chips Viviana has.\n\nWe are told:\n> Viviana has **five more chocolate chips than Susana**, and **Susana has 25 chocolate chips**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Viviana's chocolate chips} = 25 + 5 = 30\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of vanilla chips Susana has.\n\nWe are told:\n> Susana has **3/4 as many vanilla chips as Viviana**, and **Viviana has 20 vanilla chips**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Susana's vanilla chips} = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 20 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add up all the chips for both Viviana and Susana.\n\n**Viviana's chips:**\n- Chocolate: 30\n- Vanilla: 20\n- Total: $30 + 20 = 50$\n\n**Susana's chips:**\n- Chocolate: 25\n- Vanilla: 15\n- Total: $25 + 15 = 40$\n\n**Total chips together:**\n$$\n50 + 40 = 90\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 90**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Jane is awarded **10 points** for each **round** she wins.\n- Jane has a **total of 60 points**.\n- She **lost 20 points** during the game.\n- We are to find how many **rounds** she played.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the scoring system\n\n- For each **win**, Jane gets **10 points**.\n- She **lost 20 points** in total, which means she **lost 2 rounds** (since 20 \u00f7 10 = 2).\n- So, she **won 60 \u00f7 10 = 6 rounds**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total number of rounds\n\n- She **won 6 rounds**.\n- She **lost 2 rounds**.\n- So, the **total number of rounds** she played is:\n\n$$\n6 + 2 = 8\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n**Answer: 8**"
"We are given that:\n\n- The jar starts with **50 ants**.\n- The number of ants **doubles every hour**.\n- We are to find the number of ants after **5 hours**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the pattern\n\nThis is an **exponential growth** problem. The formula for exponential growth is:\n\n$$\nA = A_0 \\times 2^t\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ A $ is the final number of ants,\n- $ A_0 = 50 $ is the initial number of ants,\n- $ t = 5 $ is the time in hours,\n- $ 2^t $ represents the doubling every hour.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Plug in the values\n\n$$\nA = 50 \\times 2^5\n$$\n\n$$\n2^5 = 32\n$$\n\n$$\nA = 50 \\times 32 = 1600\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1600}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 1600**"
"Let's break this problem down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Determine how many cards Eugene used**\n\n- The deck has **52 cards**.\n- He used **all but 16** cards.\n- So, the number of cards used is:\n\n$$\n52 - 16 = 36 \\text{ cards}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Calculate the number of toothpicks used**\n\n- For **each card**, he used **75 toothpicks**.\n- So, for 36 cards:\n\n$$\n36 \\times 75 = 2700 \\text{ toothpicks}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Determine how many boxes of toothpicks he used**\n\n- Each box contains **450 toothpicks**.\n- To find how many boxes he used, divide the total toothpicks by the number per box:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2700}{450} = 6\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 6**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Understand the daily running goal\nJeff runs **for an hour (60 minutes)** each day **during weekdays**.\n\nThere are **5 weekdays**: **Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday**.\n\n### Step 2: Total minutes he should run in a week\n- 5 days \u00d7 60 minutes/day = **300 minutes**\n\n### Step 3: Adjust for Thursday and Friday\n- **Thursday**: He **cut short** his run by **20 minutes** \u2192 He ran **60 - 20 = 40 minutes**\n- **Friday**: He **jogged 10 minutes more** \u2192 He ran **60 + 10 = 70 minutes**\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the total running time for the week\n- Monday: 60 minutes  \n- Tuesday: 60 minutes  \n- Wednesday: 60 minutes  \n- Thursday: 40 minutes  \n- Friday: 70 minutes  \n\n**Total = 60 + 60 + 60 + 40 + 70 = 300 minutes**\n\n### Final Answer:\n**300 minutes** is the total time Jeff ran during the week.\n\n**Answer: 300**"
"We are told that Noemi:\n\n- Lost **$400 on roulette**  \n- Lost **$500 on blackjack**  \n- Still had **$800 left** in her purse\n\nWe need to find out how much money Noemi **began with**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total amount lost\n\nNoemi lost:\n- $400 (roulette)  \n- $500 (blackjack)  \n\nTotal lost = $400 + $500 = **$900**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total money she had at the start\n\nShe still had $800 in her purse, and she lost $900. So, the total amount she started with is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total money} = \\text{Amount lost} + \\text{Amount left} = 900 + 800 = 1700\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n**Answer: 1700**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Randy spends **$2** every time he goes to the store.\n- He makes **4 trips to the store every month**.\n- After **1 year (12 months)**, he has **$104 left**.\n- We are to find out how much money he had **at first**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate total number of trips in a year\n\nHe makes 4 trips per month, so in 12 months:\n\n$$\n4 \\text{ trips/month} \\times 12 \\text{ months} = 48 \\text{ trips}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate total amount spent in a year\n\nHe spends $2 per trip, so:\n\n$$\n48 \\text{ trips} \\times 2 \\text{ dollars/trip} = 96 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the original amount of money\n\nHe had $104 left after spending $96, so the original amount was:\n\n$$\n104 + 96 = 200 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 200**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many books Sarah bought.\n- Sarah bought **6 paperback books**.\n- Sarah bought **4 hardback books**.\n- Total books Sarah bought:  \n  $$\n  6 + 4 = 10 \\text{ books}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many books her brother bought.\n\n#### a) Paperback books:\n- Her brother bought **one-third as many paperback books as Sarah**.\n- Sarah bought 6 paperback books, so:\n  $$\n  \\frac{1}{3} \\times 6 = 2 \\text{ paperback books}\n  $$\n\n#### b) Hardback books:\n- Her brother bought **two times the number of hardback books that Sarah bought**.\n- Sarah bought 4 hardback books, so:\n  $$\n  2 \\times 4 = 8 \\text{ hardback books}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total books her brother bought:\n$$\n2 \\text{ (paperback)} + 8 \\text{ (hardback)} = 10 \\text{ books}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 10**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Recommended daily added sugar intake for men**: No more than **150 calories**.\n- **Mark's soft drink**: 2500 calories, **5%** of which is from **added sugar**.\n- **Candy bars**: Each has **25 calories of added sugar**.\n- **Mark exceeded the recommended intake by 100%**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the amount of added sugar in the soft drink\n\nThe soft drink has 2500 calories, and 5% of that is from added sugar:\n\n$$\n\\text{Added sugar in soft drink} = 2500 \\times 0.05 = 125 \\text{ calories}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the total added sugar intake\n\nMark **exceeded** the recommended 150 calories by **100%**, meaning he consumed **2 \u00d7 150 = 300 calories** of added sugar.\n\nSo, the total added sugar he consumed is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total added sugar} = 150 + 150 = 300 \\text{ calories}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Subtract the sugar from the soft drink\n\n$$\n\\text{Added sugar from candy bars} = 300 - 125 = 175 \\text{ calories}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Find how many candy bars he ate\n\nEach candy bar has 25 calories of added sugar:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of candy bars} = \\frac{175}{25} = 7\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 7}}\n$$"
"We are given the following:\n\n- The factory produces **300 gummy bears per minute**.\n- Each **packet** contains **50 gummy bears**.\n- We need to fill **240 packets**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the total number of gummy bears needed\n\nEach packet has 50 gummy bears, and we need 240 packets:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total gummy bears needed} = 240 \\times 50 = 12,000\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how long it takes to produce 12,000 gummy bears\n\nThe factory produces 300 gummy bears per minute, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Time required} = \\frac{12,000}{300} = 40 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 40**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- **Earl** has $90.\n- **Fred** has $48.\n- **Greg** has $36.\n\nDebts:\n\n- **Earl owes Fred $28**.\n- **Fred owes Greg $32**.\n- **Greg owes Earl $40**.\n\nWe need to determine how much **Greg and Earl** will have **together** **after all debts are paid**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the debts and their effects\n\nWe will process the debts one by one, updating the balances of the people involved.\n\n#### Debt 1: **Earl owes Fred $28**\n\n- Earl gives Fred $28.\n- So, **Earl's balance** decreases by $28.\n- **Fred's balance** increases by $28.\n\n**After this:**\n- Earl: $90 - $28 = **$62**\n- Fred: $48 + $28 = **$76**\n- Greg: **$36**\n\n---\n\n#### Debt 2: **Fred owes Greg $32**\n\n- Fred gives Greg $32.\n- So, **Fred's balance** decreases by $32.\n- **Greg's balance** increases by $32.\n\n**After this:**\n- Earl: **$62**\n- Fred: $76 - $32 = **$44**\n- Greg: $36 + $32 = **$68**\n\n---\n\n#### Debt 3: **Greg owes Earl $40**\n\n- Greg gives Earl $40.\n- So, **Greg's balance** decreases by $40.\n- **Earl's balance** increases by $40.\n\n**After this:**\n- Earl: $62 + $40 = **$102**\n- Fred: **$44**\n- Greg: $68 - $40"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **Toothbrushes given per patient**: 2 toothbrushes per patient.\n\n2. **Time per visit**: 0.5 hours.\n\n3. **Workday duration**: 8 hours.\n\n4. **Number of patients per day**:\n   $$\n   \\frac{8 \\text{ hours}}{0.5 \\text{ hours per visit}} = 16 \\text{ patients per day}\n   $$\n\n5. **Toothbrushes given per day**:\n   $$\n   16 \\text{ patients} \\times 2 \\text{ toothbrushes per patient} = 32 \\text{ toothbrushes per day}\n   $$\n\n6. **Number of workdays in a week**: 5 days.\n\n7. **Total toothbrushes given in a 5-day work week**:\n   $$\n   32 \\text{ toothbrushes/day} \\times 5 \\text{ days} = 160 \\text{ toothbrushes}\n   $$\n\n**Answer: 160**"
"We are given:\n\n- 5 squirrels collected **575 acorns** in total.\n- Each squirrel needs **130 acorns** to get through the winter.\n\nWe are asked to find how many **more acorns each squirrel needs to collect**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find how many acorns each squirrel has.\n\nTotal acorns collected by 5 squirrels = 575  \nSo, acorns per squirrel =  \n$$\n\\frac{575}{5} = 115\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many more acorns each squirrel needs.\n\nEach squirrel needs 130 acorns, but has only 115.  \nSo, the number of acorns each squirrel still needs is:  \n$$\n130 - 115 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:  \n**Answer: 15**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Total number of students = 25\n- Number of students who like **French fries** = 15\n- Number of students who like **burgers** = 10\n- Number of students who like **both** French fries and burgers = 6\n\nWe are to find how many students **do not like either** food.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the principle of inclusion-exclusion\n\nThe number of students who like **either** French fries or burgers is:\n\n$$\n\\text{French fries} + \\text{Burgers} - \\text{Both} = 15 + 10 - 6 = 19\n$$\n\nSo, **19 students** like **at least one** of the two foods.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Subtract from total to find those who like **neither**\n\n$$\n\\text{Students who like neither} = \\text{Total} - \\text{Students who like at least one} = 25 - 19 = 6\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 6**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Weekdays**: The coffee shop brews **10 coffee cups per hour**.\n- **Weekend**: The coffee shop brews **120 coffee cups in total over the weekend**.\n- The coffee shop is **open 5 hours a day every single day**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the number of coffee cups brewed on a weekday.\n\nThe coffee shop is open **5 hours per day**, and it brews **10 cups per hour** on weekdays.\n\n$$\n\\text{Cups per weekday} = 10 \\, \\text{cups/hour} \\times 5 \\, \\text{hours} = 50 \\, \\text{cups}\n$$\n\nThere are **5 weekdays** in a week, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total weekday cups} = 50 \\, \\text{cups/day} \\times 5 \\, \\text{days} = 250 \\, \\text{cups}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add the weekend total.\n\nWe are told the coffee shop brews **120 coffee cups in total over the weekend**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cups in a week} = 250 \\, \\text{(weekdays)} + 120 \\, \\text{(weekend)} = 370 \\, \\text{cups}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{370}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 370**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Mike ate **20** chocolate squares.\n- Jenny ate **5 more than thrice** the number of chocolate squares that Mike ate.\n\nLet's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Let the number of chocolate squares Mike ate be:\n$$\nM = 20\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Thrice the number of chocolate squares Mike ate is:\n$$\n3 \\times M = 3 \\times 20 = 60\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Jenny ate 5 more than that:\n$$\nJ = 60 + 5 = 65\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{65}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 65**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: **Red Notes (Complaints Bin)**\n\n- The red notes are in **5 rows**, with **6 notes in each row**.\n- So, the total number of **red notes** is:\n\n$$\n5 \\text{ rows} \\times 6 \\text{ notes/row} = 30 \\text{ red notes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: **Blue Notes (Compliments Bin)**\n\nWe are told:\n\n- There are **2 blue notes under each red note**.\n- There are **10 additional blue notes** scattered at the bottom.\n\n#### First, calculate the number of blue notes under the red notes:\n\n$$\n30 \\text{ red notes} \\times 2 \\text{ blue notes per red note} = 60 \\text{ blue notes}\n$$\n\n#### Then, add the 10 scattered blue notes:\n\n$$\n60 + 10 = 70 \\text{ blue notes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: **Total Notes in Both Bins**\n\n- **Red notes (complaints)**: 30\n- **Blue notes (compliments)**: 70\n\n$$\n30 + 70 = 100\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 100**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- **Bob's speed** = 6 miles per hour  \n- **Jim's speed** = 9 miles per hour  \n- **Bob has a 1-mile head start**\n\nWe want to find how many **minutes** it takes for **Jim to catch up to Bob**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the relative speed\n\nJim is faster than Bob, so he is gaining on Bob at a rate of:\n\n$$\n9 \\text{ mph} - 6 \\text{ mph} = 3 \\text{ mph}\n$$\n\nThis means Jim is closing the gap at 3 miles per hour.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the formula for time\n\n$$\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}}\n$$\n\nHere, the distance to close is **1 mile**, and the relative speed is **3 mph**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{3 \\text{ mph}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Convert hours to minutes\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes/hour} = 20 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 20**"
