"We are given the following information:\n\n1. **Franzia** is **three times as old** as **Carlo Rosi**.\n2. **Carlo Rosi** is **four times older** than **Twin Valley**.\n3. **Carlo Rosi** is **40 years old**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the age of **Franzia**\n\nFrom the first statement:\n$$\n\\text{Franzia} = 3 \\times \\text{Carlo Rosi}\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Franzia} = 3 \\times 40 = 120 \\text{ years}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the age of **Twin Valley**\n\nFrom the second statement:\n$$\n\\text{Carlo Rosi} = 4 \\times \\text{Twin Valley}\n$$\n$$\n40 = 4 \\times \\text{Twin Valley}\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Twin Valley} = \\frac{40}{4} = 10 \\text{ years}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the total age of the three brands\n\n$$\n\\text{Total age} = \\text{Franzia} + \\text{Carlo Rosi} + \\text{Twin Valley}\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Total age} = 120 + 40 + 10 = 170\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 170**"
