"We are given that a train travels **270 miles in 3 hours**. We are to find how many **additional hours** it would take to travel **an additional 180 miles** at the **same rate**.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Find the speed of the train**\n\n$$\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{270 \\text{ miles}}{3 \\text{ hours}} = 90 \\text{ miles per hour}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Find the time to travel 180 miles at the same speed**\n\n$$\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} = \\frac{180 \\text{ miles}}{90 \\text{ miles per hour}} = 2 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 2}}\n$$"
"Let's solve this step by step:\n\n1. **Marcus can fit 5 pies in his oven at once.**  \n   So, each batch has **5 pies**.\n\n2. **He bakes 7 batches of pies.**  \n   Total number of pies baked = 7 batches \u00d7 5 pies per batch = **35 pies**.\n\n3. **He slips and drops 8 of them.**  \n   So, 8 pies are dropped and presumably lost.\n\n4. **How many pies are left?**  \n   Pies left = Total pies baked \u2212 Pies dropped  \n   = 35 \u2212 8 = **27 pies**.\n\n**Answer: 27**"
