"We are given the following information:\n\n- The auto shop **buys tires as soon as cars are brought in**.\n- There are **4 cars already in the shop**.\n- **6 more customers** come in during the week.\n- Some customers **don't want any tires changed**.\n- **2 customers** only want **half the tires changed**.\n- The shop **had no tires in stock at the start of the week**.\n- At the **end of the week**, the shop has **20 tires left**.\n\nWe are to find: **How many customers decided they did not want their tires changing?**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many tires are changed per car\n\nEach car has **4 tires**.\n\n- The **4 cars already in the shop** will all have their tires changed.\n- The **6 customers** come in, and **some of them** decide not to change any tires, and **2 of them** only change **half** the tires.\n\nSo, for each customer:\n- If they **change all tires**, that's 4 tires.\n- If they **change half the tires**, that's 2 tires.\n- If they **change none**, that's 0 tires.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Let\u2019s define variables\n\nLet:\n- $ x $ = number of customers who **changed all tires**\n- $ y $ = number of customers who **changed half the tires** = 2\n- $ z $ = number of customers who **changed none** = ?\n\nWe know:\n- Total customers = 4 (already in shop) + 6 (new customers) = 10\n- So: $ x + y + z = 10 $\n- But we are told that **2 customers** only want **half** the tires changed, so $ y = 2 $\n- So: $ x + z = 10 - 2 = 8 $\n\n---\n\n###"
