"We are given the following information:\n\n- John's **normal trip** is **150 miles** and takes **3 hours**.\n- He gets **lost** and drives **50 miles out of the way**.\n- He then has to **get back on track**.\n- He **keeps the same speed** throughout the trip.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find John's speed\n\nJohn's normal trip is 150 miles in 3 hours, so his **speed** is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{150}{3} = 50 \\text{ miles per hour}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the total distance of the new trip\n\n- He drives **50 miles out of the way**.\n- Then he has to **get back on track**, which is an additional **50 miles**.\n- So the **total distance** of the new trip is:\n\n$$\n150 + 50 = 200 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the time for the new trip\n\nUsing the same speed of **50 miles per hour**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} = \\frac{200}{50} = 4 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 4}}\n$$"
"Let's break down Madeline's total expenses and savings goal first:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate total expenses\n- Rent: $1200  \n- Groceries: $400  \n- Medical expenses: $200  \n- Utilities: $60  \n\n**Total expenses** = 1200 + 400 + 200 + 60 = **$1860**\n\n### Step 2: Add the emergency savings\nShe wants to save **$200** in case of an emergency.\n\n**Total needed** = 1860 + 200 = **$2060**\n\n### Step 3: Calculate how many hours she needs to work\nShe earns **$15 per hour**.\n\nTo find the number of hours:\n$$\n\\text{Hours} = \\frac{\\text{Total needed}}{\\text{Earnings per hour}} = \\frac{2060}{15}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2060}{15} = 137.333...\n$$\n\nSince she can't work a fraction of an hour, we round **up** to the next whole number.\n\n**Answer: 138**"
