"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the money from the first 8 weeks.**\n\nFrancie gets $5 per week for 8 weeks:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 8 = 40\n$$\n\nSo, she has **$40** after 8 weeks.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the money from the next 6 weeks.**\n\nHer allowance is raised to $6 per week for 6 weeks:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 6 = 36\n$$\n\nSo, she gets **$36** in the next 6 weeks.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Total money saved.**\n\n$$\n40 + 36 = 76\n$$\n\nFrancie has **$76** in total.\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: She uses half of the money to buy new clothes.**\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 76 = 38\n$$\n\nSo, she spends **$38** on clothes.\n\n---\n\n**Step 5: Remaining money after buying clothes.**\n\n$$\n76 - 38 = 38\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 6: She buys a video game that costs $35.**\n\n$$\n38 - 35 = 3\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{3}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 3**"
"We are given a triangle with angles A, B, and C, where:\n\n- Angle A = 60\u00b0\n- Angle B = 2 \u00d7 Angle C\n\nWe are to find the measure of angle B.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the triangle angle sum property\n\nIn any triangle, the sum of the interior angles is:\n\n$$\nA + B + C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\n$$\n60^\\circ + B + C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\n$$\nB + C = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the relationship between B and C\n\nWe are told that:\n\n$$\nB = 2C\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the equation from Step 1:\n\n$$\n2C + C = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\n$$\n3C = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\n$$\nC = 40^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, find B:\n\n$$\nB = 2C = 2 \\times 40^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{80}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 80**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- There are **50 more pens than notebooks** on the shelf.\n- There are **30 notebooks** on the shelf.\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of pens.\nSince there are 50 more pens than notebooks, and there are 30 notebooks:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of pens} = 30 + 50 = 80\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Find the total number of notebooks and pens.\n$$\n\\text{Total} = \\text{Number of notebooks} + \\text{Number of pens} = 30 + 80 = 110\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 110}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- There are **20 birds**.\n- Each bird migrates **from one lake to another** in **two seasons**.\n- In the **first season**, the birds fly from **Lake Jim to Lake Disney**, which is **50 miles apart**.\n- In the **second season**, they fly from **Lake Disney to Lake London**, which is **60 miles apart**.\n\n### Step 1: Distance per bird for two seasons\n- First season: 50 miles\n- Second season: 60 miles\n- Total distance per bird = 50 + 60 = **110 miles**\n\n### Step 2: Total distance for all 20 birds\n- Total distance = 20 birds \u00d7 110 miles/bird = **2200 miles**\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 2200**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. The **sum of the ages of Jeremy, Sebastian, and Sophia in three years** is **150**.\n2. **Currently**, **Sebastian is 4 years older than Jeremy**.\n3. **Currently**, **Jeremy is 40 years old**.\n\nWe are to find **Sophia's age three years from now**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find Sebastian's current age\n\nWe are told:\n- Jeremy is currently **40**.\n- Sebastian is **4 years older** than Jeremy.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{Sebastian's current age} = 40 + 4 = 44\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Let Sophia's current age be $ S $\n\nWe are told that in **three years**, the **sum of their ages** will be **150**.\n\nIn three years:\n- Jeremy's age = $ 40 + 3 = 43 $\n- Sebastian's age = $ 44 + 3 = 47 $\n- Sophia's age = $ S + 3 $\n\nSo the sum of their ages in three years is:\n$$\n(43) + (47) + (S + 3) = 150\n$$\n\nSimplify:\n$$\n43 + 47 + S + 3 = 150\n$$\n$$\n93 + S = 150\n$$\n$$\nS = 150 - 93 = 57\n$$\n\nSo Sophia is **57 years old now**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find Sophia's age **three years from now**\n\n$$\n\\text{Sophia's age in 3 years} = 57 + 3 = 60\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 60**"
"Step 1: Sonny received 45 boxes of cookies.  \nStep 2: He gave away 12 to his brother, 9 to his sister, and 7 to his cousin.  \nTotal given away = 12 + 9 + 7 = 28 boxes.  \nStep 3: Subtract the boxes given away from the total:  \n45 - 28 = 17.  \n\nAnswer: 17."
"We are given the following:\n\n- **Rabbit's speed** = 25 miles per hour  \n- **Cat's speed** = 20 miles per hour  \n- **Cat gets a 15-minute head start** = 0.25 hours  \n\nWe want to find how long it will take for the **rabbit to catch up** to the **cat**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the head start distance of the cat\n\nThe cat runs for 0.25 hours at 20 mph:\n\n$$\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 20 \\times 0.25 = 5 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\nSo, the cat is **5 miles ahead** when the rabbit starts running.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Let $ t $ be the time (in hours) it takes for the rabbit to catch up\n\nIn that time:\n\n- The **rabbit** runs: $ 25t $ miles  \n- The **cat** runs: $ 20t $ miles, **plus the 5-mile head start**\n\nSo, the rabbit catches up when:\n\n$$\n25t = 20t + 5\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Solve the equation\n\n$$\n25t = 20t + 5 \\\\\n25t - 20t = 5 \\\\\n5t = 5 \\\\\nt = 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 1**"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Total number of people expected to visit\n\n- **50 people** are known to show up.\n- **40 people** are **hopes** to show up (i.e., **possibly** show up).\n- So, the **total number of people** that **could** visit is:\n\n$$\n50 + 40 = 90 \\text{ people}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Number of gift bags already made\n\n- **10 extravagant gift bags** for the first 10 people.\n- **20 average gift bags** for 20 people.\n\nSo, total bags already made:\n\n$$\n10 + 20 = 30 \\text{ bags}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: How many bags are needed in total?\n\nSince **90 people** could visit, and **each person** needs **one gift bag**, he needs:\n\n$$\n90 \\text{ bags}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: How many more bags does he need to make?\n\n$$\n90 - 30 = 60 \\text{ more bags}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 60**  \n**Answer: 60**"
"Step 1: Find the number of pages Maya read last week.  \nShe read 5 books, each with 300 pages:  \n$ 5 \\times 300 = 1500 $ pages.\n\nStep 2: Find the number of pages Maya read this week.  \nThis week, she read twice as much as last week:  \n$ 2 \\times 1500 = 3000 $ pages.\n\nStep 3: Add the pages from both weeks to find the total.  \n$ 1500 + 3000 = 4500 $ pages.\n\nAnswer: 4500."
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Emma is **7 years old**.\n- Emma's sister is **9 years older** than Emma.\n- We are to find **how old Emma will be when her sister is 56**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the current age of Emma's sister.\n\nEmma is 7 years old, and her sister is 9 years older:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sister's current age} = 7 + 9 = 16\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of years until the sister is 56.\n\n$$\n\\text{Years until sister is 56} = 56 - 16 = 40\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find Emma's age in 40 years.\n\n$$\n\\text{Emma's future age} = 7 + 40 = 47\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 47**  \nAnswer: 47"
"We are given the number of billboards Benjamin saw in each hour:\n\n- **First hour**: 17 billboards  \n- **Second hour**: 20 billboards  \n- **Third hour**: 23 billboards  \n\nTo find the **average number of billboards per hour**, we use the formula for average:\n\n$$\n\\text{Average} = \\frac{\\text{Sum of all billboards}}{\\text{Number of hours}}\n$$\n\n### Step 1: Add the number of billboards seen in each hour\n\n$$\n17 + 20 + 23 = 60\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Divide by the number of hours (3)\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{3} = 20\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 20**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Milo's downhill skateboard speed** is **twice** his running speed.\n- **Cory's wheelchair speed** is **twice** Milo's skateboard speed.\n- Cory always drives his wheelchair at **12 miles per hour**.\n\nWe are to find: **How many miles can Milo run in two hours?**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Define variables\n\nLet:\n- $ R $ = Milo's running speed (in mph)\n- Then, Milo's skateboard speed = $ 2R $\n- Cory's wheelchair speed = $ 2 \\times \\text{Milo's skateboard speed} = 2(2R) = 4R $\n\nWe are told that Cory's wheelchair speed is **12 mph**.\n\nSo:\n$$\n4R = 12 \\Rightarrow R = 3 \\text{ mph}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find how many miles Milo can run in 2 hours\n\nMilo's running speed = 3 mph  \nTime = 2 hours\n\n$$\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 3 \\times 2 = 6 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 6**"
"Step 1: Find how many berries one bird eats in a day.  \n**One bird eats 7 berries per day.**\n\nStep 2: Find how many berries 5 birds eat in a day.  \n**5 birds \u00d7 7 berries/day = 35 berries/day.**\n\nStep 3: Find how many berries 5 birds eat in 4 days.  \n**35 berries/day \u00d7 4 days = 140 berries.**\n\nAnswer: 140."
"We are given the following information:\n\n1. After Raj cut some bananas from the tree, **100 bananas remain on the tree**.\n2. Raj has **eaten 70 bananas**.\n3. Raj has **twice as many bananas remaining in his basket as he has eaten**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many bananas Raj has left in his basket.\n\nRaj has eaten 70 bananas, and he has **twice as many remaining** in his basket.\n\nSo, bananas remaining in his basket =  \n$$\n2 \\times 70 = 140\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total number of bananas Raj has taken from the tree.\n\nRaj has eaten 70 bananas and has 140 left in his basket, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total bananas Raj took} = 70 + 140 = 210\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total number of bananas on the tree initially.\n\nThe tree had 100 bananas left **after** Raj cut some, and he took 210 bananas from the tree. So:\n\n$$\n\\text{Initial number of bananas on the tree} = 100 + 210 = 310\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer: 310**  \nAnswer: 310"
"Let's break this down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Total time spent scuba diving\nJim spends **8 hours** scuba diving.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Gold coins found in the treasure chest\nHe finds **100 gold coins** in the treasure chest.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Gold coins in the smaller bags\nHe finds **2 smaller bags**, and each has **half as much gold as the treasure chest**.\n\nSo, each smaller bag has:\n$$\n\\frac{100}{2} = 50 \\text{ gold coins}\n$$\n\nTotal in both bags:\n$$\n2 \\times 50 = 100 \\text{ gold coins}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total gold found\n- Treasure chest: 100 coins\n- Two smaller bags: 100 coins\n\nTotal gold:\n$$\n100 + 100 = 200 \\text{ gold coins}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Gold per hour\nHe spent **8 hours** and found **200 gold coins**.\n\nSo, gold per hour:\n$$\n\\frac{200}{8} = 25 \\text{ gold coins per hour}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 25**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The **capacity** of the sandbag is **250 pounds**.\n- The **bag is filled 80% full**.\n- The **filling material is 40% heavier than sand**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the weight of the sand in the bag\n\nIf the bag is **80% full**, then the **weight of the sand** in the bag is:\n\n$$\n0.8 \\times 250 = 200 \\text{ pounds}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Account for the fact that the material is 40% heavier than sand\n\nThis means the **filling material weighs 140% of the weight of sand** (since 100% + 40% = 140%).\n\nSo, the **actual weight of the filling material** is:\n\n$$\n200 \\times 1.4 = 280 \\text{ pounds}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 280}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Michael's hole depth**: 400 feet less than **twice** the depth of his father's hole.\n- **Father's digging rate**: 4 feet per hour.\n- **Father's digging time**: 400 hours.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the depth of the father's hole\n\nThe father dug at a rate of 4 feet per hour for 400 hours:\n\n$$\n\\text{Depth of father's hole} = 4 \\, \\text{ft/h} \\times 400 \\, \\text{h} = 1600 \\, \\text{feet}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the depth of Michael's hole\n\nMichael's hole is 400 feet less than **twice** the depth of his father's hole:\n\n$$\n\\text{Depth of Michael's hole} = 2 \\times 1600 - 400 = 3200 - 400 = 2800 \\, \\text{feet}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the time it takes Michael to dig his hole\n\nMichael works at the **same rate** as his father: 4 feet per hour.\n\n$$\n\\text{Time for Michael} = \\frac{2800 \\, \\text{ft}}{4 \\, \\text{ft/h}} = 700 \\, \\text{hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 700**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The store has **500 cars** for sale.\n- There are **10 sales professionals**.\n- Each salesperson sells **10 cars per month**.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of cars sold per month.\n\nEach salesperson sells 10 cars per month, and there are 10 sales professionals:\n\n$$\n10 \\text{ salespeople} \\times 10 \\text{ cars per salesperson per month} = 100 \\text{ cars per month}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many months it will take to sell all 500 cars.\n\n$$\n\\frac{500 \\text{ cars}}{100 \\text{ cars per month}} = 5 \\text{ months}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 5**"
"We are given the answers to three math problems that August solved on his calculator. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Define the answers\n\nLet:\n- The **first** answer be **A** = 600 (given).\n- The **second** answer is **twice** the first:  \n  $$\n  B = 2 \\times A = 2 \\times 600 = 1200\n  $$\n- The **third** answer is **400 less than the combined total** of the first and second answers:  \n  $$\n  C = (A + B) - 400 = (600 + 1200) - 400 = 1800 - 400 = 1400\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add the three answers together\n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = A + B + C = 600 + 1200 + 1400 = 3200\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{3200}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The New York City Council bought **200 streetlights**.\n- There are **15 squares** (parks) in New York City.\n- Each park will have **12 new streetlights**.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of streetlights used.\n\nEach of the 15 parks will have 12 streetlights:\n\n$$\n15 \\text{ parks} \\times 12 \\text{ streetlights per park} = 180 \\text{ streetlights}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Subtract the number of used streetlights from the total bought.\n\n$$\n200 \\text{ total streetlights} - 180 \\text{ used streetlights} = 20 \\text{ unused streetlights}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 20}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Trevor spends **$80** every year.\n- Trevor always spends **$20 more** than Reed.\n- Reed spends **2 times** as much as Quinn.\n\nWe are to find the **total amount** spent by **Trevor, Reed, and Quinn** in **4 years**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let\u2019s define variables\n\nLet:\n- T = amount Trevor spends per year = **$80**\n- R = amount Reed spends per year\n- Q = amount Quinn spends per year\n\nFrom the problem:\n\n- **T = R + $20** \u2192 So, **R = T - $20 = 80 - 20 = $60**\n- **R = 2 \u00d7 Q** \u2192 So, **Q = R / 2 = 60 / 2 = $30**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total spent by all three in one year\n\nTrevor: $80  \nReed: $60  \nQuinn: $30  \n\nTotal per year = 80 + 60 + 30 = **$170**\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Total spent in 4 years\n\nTotal = 170 \u00d7 4 = **$680**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 680**"
"We are given the rates at which Jane, Kyla, and Anthony can fold towels, and we need to find how many towels they can fold together in **one hour**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the rate of each person (towels per minute)\n\n#### Jane:\n- Folds 3 towels in 5 minutes.\n- Rate = $ \\frac{3}{5} $ towels per minute.\n\n#### Kyla:\n- Folds 5 towels in 10 minutes.\n- Rate = $ \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2} $ towels per minute.\n\n#### Anthony:\n- Folds 7 towels in 20 minutes.\n- Rate = $ \\frac{7}{20} $ towels per minute.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add the rates together\n\n$$\n\\text{Total rate} = \\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{7}{20}\n$$\n\nFind a common denominator (20):\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{12}{20}, \\quad \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{10}{20}, \\quad \\frac{7}{20} = \\frac{7}{20}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Total rate} = \\frac{12 + 10 + 7}{20} = \\frac{29}{20} \\text{ towels per minute}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Multiply by 60 minutes to get the number of towels folded in 1 hour\n\n$$\n\\text{Towels in 1 hour} = \\frac{29}{20} \\times 60 = \\frac{29 \\times 60}{20} = 29 \\times 3 = 87\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Xia shared **100 stickers** with her friends.\n- After sharing, she had **5 sheets of stickers left**.\n- Each sheet had **10 stickers** on it.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of stickers in the 5 sheets\n\nEach sheet has 10 stickers, so:\n\n$$\n5 \\text{ sheets} \\times 10 \\text{ stickers per sheet} = 50 \\text{ stickers}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add the stickers she shared to the stickers she had left\n\nShe shared 100 stickers, and had 50 stickers left, so:\n\n$$\n100 + 50 = 150 \\text{ stickers}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{150}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 150**"
"**Step 1: Calculate the distance Tamika drove.**  \nTamika drove for 8 hours at 45 mph.  \nDistance = speed \u00d7 time  \n= 45 mph \u00d7 8 hours  \n= **360 miles**\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the distance Logan drove.**  \nLogan drove for 5 hours at 55 mph.  \nDistance = speed \u00d7 time  \n= 55 mph \u00d7 5 hours  \n= **275 miles**\n\n**Step 3: Find the difference in distance.**  \nDifference = 360 miles \u2013 275 miles  \n= **85 miles**\n\n**Answer: 85**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- James earns **$0.50 per mile**.\n- He pays **$4.00 per gallon** for gas.\n- His truck gets **20 miles per gallon**.\n- The trip is **600 miles** long.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate total earnings from driving\n\nJames earns $0.50 per mile, and the trip is 600 miles.\n\n$$\n\\text{Earnings} = 0.50 \\times 600 = 300 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the amount of gas used\n\nThe truck gets 20 miles per gallon, so for 600 miles:\n\n$$\n\\text{Gallons used} = \\frac{600}{20} = 30 \\text{ gallons}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate total gas cost\n\nGas costs $4.00 per gallon, so:\n\n$$\n\\text{Gas cost} = 4.00 \\times 30 = 120 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Calculate profit\n\n$$\n\\text{Profit} = \\text{Earnings} - \\text{Gas cost} = 300 - 120 = 180 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 180**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **Monday**:\n  - 4 poodles\n  - 2 Chihuahuas\n\n- **Tuesday**:\n  - 2 Chihuahuas (same as Monday)\n  - **x poodles** (unknown, this is what we need to find)\n\n- **Wednesday**:\n  - 4 Labradors\n\n**Time per dog**:\n- Poodle: 2 hours\n- Chihuahua: 1 hour\n- Labrador: 3 hours\n\n**Total time available**: 32 hours\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the time spent on Monday\n\n- 4 poodles \u00d7 2 hours = 8 hours\n- 2 Chihuahuas \u00d7 1 hour = 2 hours  \n**Total Monday time** = 8 + 2 = **10 hours**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the time spent on Tuesday\n\n- 2 Chihuahuas \u00d7 1 hour = 2 hours\n- x poodles \u00d7 2 hours = 2x hours  \n**Total Tuesday time** = 2 + 2x hours\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the time spent on Wednesday\n\n- 4 Labradors \u00d7 3 hours = 12 hours\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Add up the total time\n\nTotal time = Monday + Tuesday + Wednesday  \n= 10 + (2 + 2x) + 12  \n= 10 + 2 + 2x + 12  \n= 24 + 2x\n\nWe are told the total time is 32 hours:\n\n$$\n24 + 2x = 32\n$$\n\nSubtract 24 from both sides:\n\n$$\n2x = 8\n$$\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n$$\nx = 4\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n**Charlotte can"
"Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: KILOMETERS RIDDEN ON EACH DAY\n\n- **Monday**: 40 km  \n- **Tuesday**: 50 km  \n- **Wednesday**: 50% fewer than Tuesday  \n  - 50% of 50 km = 25 km  \n  - So, Wednesday = 50 km - 25 km = **30 km**  \n- **Thursday**: Sum of Monday and Wednesday  \n  - Monday = 40 km  \n  - Wednesday = 30 km  \n  - So, Thursday = 40 km + 30 km = **70 km**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Total Kilometers\n\nAdd up the kilometers for each day:\n\n- Monday: 40 km  \n- Tuesday: 50 km  \n- Wednesday: 30 km  \n- Thursday: 70 km  \n\n**Total = 40 + 50 + 30 + 70 = 190 km**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 190**"
"Let's break this down step by step:\n\n1. **Bruce's goals**: Bruce scored **4 goals**.\n\n2. **Michael's goals**: The problem says Michael scored **3 times more than Bruce**.  \n   - \"3 times more than Bruce\" means Michael scored **3 \u00d7 Bruce's goals**.  \n   - So, Michael scored:  \n     $ 3 \\times 4 = 12 $ goals.\n\n3. **Total goals**:  \n   Add Bruce's and Michael's goals together:  \n   $ 4 + 12 = 16 $.\n\n**Answer: 16**."
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The total size of the game: **880 MB**\n- Amount already downloaded: **310 MB**\n- New download speed: **3 MB/minute**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the remaining data to download\n\n$$\n\\text{Remaining data} = 880 - 310 = 570 \\text{ MB}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the time needed to download the remaining data\n\n$$\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Remaining data}}{\\text{Download speed}} = \\frac{570}{3} = 190 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 190**"
"Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total number of hushpuppies needed.**\n\n- Each guest eats 5 hushpuppies.\n- There are 20 guests.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total hushpuppies} = 20 \\times 5 = 100\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Determine how many batches of hushpuppies are needed.**\n\n- Walter can cook 10 hushpuppies in 8 minutes.\n- So, each batch = 10 hushpuppies.\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of batches} = \\frac{100}{10} = 10\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total time to cook all the batches.**\n\n- Each batch takes 8 minutes.\n- There are 10 batches.\n\n$$\n\\text{Total time} = 10 \\times 8 = 80 \\text{ minutes}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 80**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- There are **3 times as many grape candies as cherry candies**.\n- There are **2 times as many apple candies as grape candies**.\n- Each candy costs **$2.50**.\n- The **total cost** of all the candies is **$200**.\n\nLet\u2019s define variables:\n\n- Let $ C $ = number of **cherry candies**.\n- Then, the number of **grape candies** is $ 3C $.\n- The number of **apple candies** is $ 2 \\times 3C = 6C $.\n\n### Step 1: Total number of candies\nTotal number of candies = $ C + 3C + 6C = 10C $\n\n### Step 2: Total cost\nEach candy costs $2.50, so total cost is:\n\n$$\n10C \\times 2.50 = 200\n$$\n\n### Step 3: Solve for $ C $\n\n$$\n25C = 200\n$$\n$$\nC = \\frac{200}{25} = 8\n$$\n\n### Step 4: Find the number of grape candies\n\n$$\n\\text{Grape candies} = 3C = 3 \\times 8 = 24\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 24**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- **On a cold day (50\u00b0F):**  \n  Peyton throws the ball **20 yards** per throw.  \n  He threw the ball **20 times**.  \n  So, total yards on that day = 20 throws \u00d7 20 yards = **400 yards**.\n\n- **On a warm day (80\u00b0F):**  \n  The ball travels **twice as far** as it does at 50\u00b0F.  \n  So, at 80\u00b0F, each throw is **40 yards**.  \n  He threw the ball **30 times**.  \n  So, total yards on that day = 30 throws \u00d7 40 yards = **1200 yards**.\n\nNow, add the two days together:\n\n**Total yards = 400 + 1200 = 1600 yards**\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 1600**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- The total distance between two towns is **200 miles**.\n- Roger and his friend drove **1/4 of the total distance** in **1 hour**.\n- They took **1 hour** for lunch.\n- Then they drove the **remaining distance** at the **same speed** as before.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the distance driven in the first part\n\nThey drove **1/4 of the total distance**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Distance}_1 = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 200 = 50 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\nThey drove this 50 miles in **1 hour**, so their **speed** is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{50 \\text{ miles}}{1 \\text{ hour}} = 50 \\text{ miles per hour}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the remaining distance\n\n$$\n\\text{Remaining distance} = 200 - 50 = 150 \\text{ miles}\n$$\n\nThey drive this at the **same speed** of **50 miles per hour**, so the time taken for the second part is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Time}_2 = \\frac{150 \\text{ miles}}{50 \\text{ miles/hour}} = 3 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add up all the time\n\n- Time for first part: **1 hour**\n- Lunch: **1 hour**\n- Time for second part: **3 hours**\n\n$$\n\\text{Total time} = 1 + 1 + 3 = 5 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 5**"
"Let's break down the problem step by step and calculate the total number of animals Josie counted.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Define the variables\n\nLet:\n- $ A $ = number of **antelopes** = 80\n- $ R $ = number of **rabbits**\n- $ H $ = number of **hyenas**\n- $ W $ = number of **wild dogs**\n- $ L $ = number of **leopards**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the given information to find each value\n\n1. **Antelopes** = 80  \n   $ A = 80 $\n\n2. **Rabbits** = 34 more than antelopes  \n   $ R = A + 34 = 80 + 34 = 114 $\n\n3. **Hyenas** = 42 fewer than the total number of antelopes and rabbits combined  \n   $ H = (A + R) - 42 = (80 + 114) - 42 = 194 - 42 = 152 $\n\n4. **Wild dogs** = 50 more than hyenas  \n   $ W = H + 50 = 152 + 50 = 202 $\n\n5. **Leopards** = half the number of rabbits  \n   $ L = \\frac{R}{2} = \\frac{114}{2} = 57 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Add all the animals together\n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = A + R + H + W + L\n$$\n$$\n= 80 + 114 + 152 + 202 + 57\n$$\n\nNow, add them step by step:\n\n- $ 80 + 114 = 194 $\n- $ "
"We are told that Allison uploads **10 one-hour videos** each day, and she did this **halfway through June**, then **doubled the number of video hours** on the **remaining days**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the number of days in June\n\nJune has **30 days**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many days she uploaded 10 hours\n\nShe uploaded 10 hours per day **halfway through June**. That means she uploaded for:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{2} = 15 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\nSo, for the first 15 days, she uploaded:\n\n$$\n15 \\text{ days} \\times 10 \\text{ hours/day} = 150 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine the number of days she uploaded 20 hours\n\nAfter the first 15 days, she **doubled** the number of hours per day, so she uploaded:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 2 = 20 \\text{ hours/day}\n$$\n\nShe did this for the remaining:\n\n$$\n30 - 15 = 15 \\text{ days}\n$$\n\nSo, for the last 15 days, she uploaded:\n\n$$\n15 \\text{ days} \\times 20 \\text{ hours/day} = 300 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Add the total hours\n\n$$\n150 \\text{ hours} + 300 \\text{ hours} = 450 \\text{ hours}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 450**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Carla needs to collect **30 leaves** and **20 bugs**.\n- The project is due in **10 days**.\n- Carla wants to collect the **same number of items each day**.\n\n### Step 1: Find the total number of items to collect\n- Leaves: 30\n- Bugs: 20  \nTotal items = 30 + 20 = **50 items**\n\n### Step 2: Divide the total number of items by the number of days\n- 50 items \u00f7 10 days = **5 items per day**\n\n### Final Answer:\n**Answer: 5**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Edward needs **40 feet of copper pipe**.\n- For **every 5 feet** of pipe, he uses **1 tightening bolt**.\n- For **every bolt**, he uses **2 washers**.\n- He buys a **bag of 20 washers**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine how many bolts Edward uses\n\nHe uses **1 bolt for every 5 feet** of pipe.\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of bolts} = \\frac{40}{5} = 8\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many washers he uses\n\nHe uses **2 washers per bolt**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of washers used} = 8 \\times 2 = 16\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine how many washers are left\n\nHe started with **20 washers**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Washers remaining} = 20 - 16 = 4\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 4**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- Nancy drinks **54 pounds** of water per day.\n- This is **60%** of her body weight.\n\nWe are to find **Nancy's body weight**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Let her body weight be $ x $ pounds.\n\nAccording to the problem:\n\n$$\n60\\% \\text{ of } x = 54\n$$\n\n$$\n0.6x = 54\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve for $ x $\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{54}{0.6}\n$$\n\n$$\nx = 90\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{90}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 90**"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- A full-size Mustang is **240 inches** long.\n- The **mid-size model** is **1/10th** the size of the full-size Mustang.\n- The **smallest model** is **half the size** of the mid-size model.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the length of the mid-size model\n\nThe mid-size model is **1/10th** the size of the full-size Mustang:\n\n$$\n\\text{Mid-size length} = \\frac{1}{10} \\times 240 = 24 \\text{ inches}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the length of the smallest model\n\nThe smallest model is **half the size** of the mid-size model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Smallest model length} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 24 = 12 \\text{ inches}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\text{Answer: 12}}\n$$"
"We are given the following information:\n\n- Taylor has **4 pets**.\n- **3 of his friends** each have **twice as many pets as Taylor**.\n- **Another 2 friends** each have **2 pets**.\n\nWe need to find the **total number of pets** that Taylor and all his friends have together.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Taylor's pets\nTaylor has **4 pets**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Pets of the first 3 friends\n\nEach of these 3 friends has **twice as many pets as Taylor**.\n\n- Taylor has 4 pets \u2192 Each of these 3 friends has $ 2 \\times 4 = 8 $ pets.\n- So, total pets from these 3 friends = $ 3 \\times 8 = 24 $ pets.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Pets of the next 2 friends\n\nEach of these 2 friends has **2 pets**.\n\n- Total pets from these 2 friends = $ 2 \\times 2 = 4 $ pets.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Total number of pets\n\nAdd up all the pets:\n\n- Taylor: 4 pets  \n- 3 friends with 8 pets each: 24 pets  \n- 2 friends with 2 pets each: 4 pets  \n\n$$\n\\text{Total} = 4 + 24 + 4 = 32\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 32**"
